ease view from your car\n7. HIDDEN LAKE See where water samping\nfrom the red sandstone has formed an under\nground late inaide e cave. Don\'t plan to\nhike up? You will need 4WD.\nREST\n8. INDIAN RUINS\nThe earliest humans came to Angel Canyon\n11,000 years ago. The Anasazi left ruins and\nwritings ca asidence they Svadbare.\nANGELS\n9. ANGELS REST At this scenic place of\nburial and remembrance, celebrate the\nlasting bond we shore with animals.\nKANA\nKanab Canyon\nYONG\nang tai ne vis\n11,\n●Labyrinth\n10.\nAngels\nOverlook\nTurtle Rock\nTEMA\nUT.\nSAPP\nPetroglyph Rock\n10\n6.\në\non Roa\nVan\nTurtle\nReck\n(from her)\n9.\nAng Rant\nN\nHidden Lake Walking Trail\n(1.5 million)\n8.\nIndian Ruins\nHidden Lake\nBest\nFriends\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY SPOILER\n10. ANGELS OVERLOCK The overlook is\nthe final resting place for many beloved pets.\nCheck out the breathtaking views.\n11. LABYRINTH Explore the labyrinth\nbeautiful, peaceful, relaxing and inspiring.\nDesigned by Best Friends cofounder Cyrus\nMejia\n12. ANGELS OVERLOOK TRAIL\nTake Angels Overlock Tail to Angel Village\nfor lunch.\n13. ANGEL VILLAGE LUNCHROOM\nVeger lunch ($5 buffer and salas berl is served\nevery day, 11:30 am to 1:00 am. Stop in\nanytime during business hours to enjoy the\nbreathtaking views or the rotation exhibits.','{\n   \"trail_name\": \"Best Friends Animal Sanctuary\",\n   \"distance\": null,\n   \"best_season\": null,\n   \"difficulty\": null,\n   \"elevation\": null,\n   \"latitude\": null,\n   \"longitude\": null,\n   \"accessibility\": null,\n   \"pets_allowed\": null,\n   \"reservation_required\": null,\n   \"average_hours_to_hike\": null,\n   \"vehicle\": null,\n   \"getting_there\": null,\n   \"description\": \"Best Friends Animal Sanctuary is a home to around 1,700 animals, providing love, care, and the opportunity to find families. This sanctuary offers areas to explore on your own pace. Visitors can volunteer, donate, or adopt animals. The sanctuary also features a Welcome Center, a food truck, hiking trails, rock formations, Indian ruins, and scenic overlooks.\",\n   \"description_html\": \"<p>Best Friends Animal Sanctuary is home to around 1,700 animals, providing love, care, and the opportunity to find families. This sanctuary offers areas to explore on your own pace. Visitors can volunteer, donate, or adopt animals. The sanctuary also features a Welcome Center, a food truck, hiking trails, rock formations, Indian ruins, and scenic overlooks.</p>\",\n   \"additional_info\": \"Visitors are asked to refrain from driving around the animal areas for the animals\' comfort and privacy. Guided Sanctuary Tours are offered at the Welcome Center. The food truck offers vegan meals for $5. Some hiking trails require 4WD vehicles. The sanctuary is located in Kanab, Utah.\"\n}','2023-09-22 02:49:46','2023-09-22 02:54:38'),(10,'trails',265,'',NULL,'CORAL PINK SAND DUNES\nSTATE PARK\nDESCANSAT\nASMEN\nGPS 37 2.239\n112 43 928\n5 mi\nCORAL PINK\nP BAND DUNES\nSTATE PARK\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nADVENTURE MAP\n网络弛牙口式\nBEST SEASON:\nOFF ROAD VEHICLE:\nCAMPGROUND:\nELEVATION:\nAVERAGE TIME: 1 HOUR - FULL DAY\nDIFFICULTY:\nCORAL PINK SAND DUNES\nSTATE PARK\nYEAR ROUND\n15 mi\nNO\n9.5 mi\nPONDEROSA GROVE\nCAMPGROUND\nGETTING THERE:\n•From Kanab, drve 8 miles north en HWY 89, turn left after mile pest 71 ant Hanrock 2nad.\nTravel 9.5 miles, at the stop sign turn lef.\n• The park entrance will be 5 miles on the lett.\n• There are two campgrounds, one at the Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park and the Ponderasa\nGrove Compgrourd on the Hancock Road,\nSOUTH PORK\nNDIAN CANYON\nDHV\n• Sardboards can be rented and ATV and repelling tours can be arranged at the Sand Dunes\nState Park,\nYES\n• After a long exciting day of playing of the dunes, make your way back to Kanob, Bose Comp\nfor adventure seekers. Reserve a table for dining and enjoy the atmosphere of one of our\npopular restaurants. We are sure you will discover one of the best dining experience in\nSouthern Utah.\n• See the Kane County Visitor Center for additional maps and adventures in the area.\n5.4 mi\n6000 FEET\nDISCLAIMER:\nThis map and directions are intended for geroral reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads are impassable when wet. Please respect\nPrivale Properly. Be prepared for u wilderness experience. Always bring esha waler,\nfood, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non existent in most areas\nDo not remove ortifacts or deface the \'rock on\'. Lecve no trace of your visit so\nothers may enjoy.\nKanab Visitor\'s Center 78 S 100 E\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\ntread lightly!\nEASY\nHELL DONE\nPICTOGRAPH\nThe Coral Pink Sand Dures State Park is a\nlocal favorite. Let your imagination run wild.\nYour adventure possibilities are endless here,\nso let your imagination run wild, Ventur\nonto the shifting sea of pink sand by\nfoot, bike, board or OHV. You will discover.\n1200 acres of open r dirg areas for OHV\nenthusiasts. Adjacent to the park, riders\nwill find hundreds of miles of\nadditional mails on BLM kand. Vis turs can\nexplore the South Fork Indian Canyon\nPictographs, Hell Dive Pictographs, just\n4 mies northeast of the pork are\ndinosaur tracks. Bring a lunch and enjoy the\npicnic ac lities and the view from the\nObservation Deck\nPlay in the sand and become a kid again.\nHANCOCK ROAD\n7\nLIFE\nUTAH\nFLEVATED\n(7.8 mi\nMILE\nARTINY\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILED\nRAPP\nspant\n(89)\nN\nKANAB\nKANAB\nMP','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park\",\n  \"distance\": \"5 mi\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year-round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n  \"elevation\": \"6000 FEET\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.040398\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-112.732130\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"Yes\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"No\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"1-8 hours\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"Off-road vehicle\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 8 miles north on HWY 89, turn left after mile post 71 and onto Hancock Road. Travel 9.5 miles, at the stop sign turn left. The park entrance will be 5 miles on the left.\",\n  \"description\": \"The Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park is a local favorite. Let your imagination run wild. Your adventure possibilities are endless here, so let your imagination run wild. Venture onto the shifting sea of pink sand by foot, bike, board, or OHV. You will discover 1200 acres of open riding areas for OHV enthusiasts. Adjacent to the park, riders will find hundreds of miles of additional trails on BLM land. Visitors can explore the South Fork Indian Canyon Pictographs, Hell Dive Pictographs, just 4 miles northeast of the park, and dinosaur tracks. Bring a lunch and enjoy the picnic facilities and the view from the Observation Deck. Play in the sand and become a kid again.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>The Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park is a local favorite. Let your imagination run wild. Your adventure possibilities are endless here, so let your imagination run wild. Venture onto the shifting sea of pink sand by foot, bike, board, or OHV. You will discover 1200 acres of open riding areas for OHV enthusiasts. Adjacent to the park, riders will find hundreds of miles of additional trails on BLM land. Visitors can explore the South Fork Indian Canyon Pictographs, Hell Dive Pictographs, just 4 miles northeast of the park, and dinosaur tracks. Bring a lunch and enjoy the picnic facilities and the view from the Observation Deck. Play in the sand and become a kid again.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"After a long exciting day of playing on the dunes, make your way back to Kanab for adventure seekers. Reserve a table for dining and enjoy the atmosphere of one of our popular restaurants. We are sure you will discover one of the best dining experiences in Southern Utah. See the Kane County Visitor Center for additional maps and adventures in the area.\",\n}','2023-09-22 02:49:57','2023-09-22 02:54:59'),(11,'trails',266,'',NULL,'-\nDINOSAUR TRACKS\nHIKE\nHIKE\nMENEM\nN\nCanyon\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBEST SEASON:\nVEHICLE:\nHIXING:\nAVERAGE TIME\nDIFFICULTY:\nELEVATION:\nDINOSAUR TRACKS\nC\nDISCLAIMER:\nMILE\n6\n7\nGETTING THERE\n• From Kanal, travel north on Ilwy 09, approximately\n3 miles.\n• Between milepost 67 and 68, turn right at the Port of Enty.\nPark at the south end by the porta potty.\n• Walk south through the sage brush until you come to an old dirt road and head east.\nFollow that for a short distance until you see on opening in the rocks that will take you\nup to the next level. You will see a faint trail that follows the ledge to the right.\n• After a short distance the trail will meander up the hill until you reach the top.\n• Start looking on the ground for the dinosaur tracks and enjoy the great views.\nNORTH\n89\nThis map and diredions ure intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads are impassabe when wet. Please respost\nFrivote Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water\nfond, sunscreen, and supplies Cell phone service is non existent in most areas\nDo not remova artifacts or deface the \'rock art, cave no race of your visit so\nothers may enjoy.\"\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 $ 100 E\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nPORT OF GPS 37 11.4213\nENTRY 112 38.734\nDINOSAUR\nTRACKS\n3.4 mi\nYEAR ROUND\nANY\n1 MILE ROUND TRIP\n2 HOURS\nSTEEP AND ROCKY\nKANAB\nNO\nADVENTURE MAP tread lightly!\nv\n5700 FEET\nCARVED\nOver 20 species of cinosaurs have\nbeen discovered in this area. 18.5\nmillion-year-old tracks from different\ntypes of animals are well preserved\nhere in sick rock outcrops, such as\ntracks from a six footlong\nccrnivorous dinosaur (Grallator),\nand a six-foot land dwelling crocodile\nto name a few. The trail is steep and\nrocky to the top. Watch your sup as\nyou go. Rattlesnakes have been\nseen in the area, watch your step.\nWhen you reach the top of the trail,\nyou will see a couple of large flat\nrocks resembling a table tcp. Loak\ncarefully and you can ind many\ntracks, mostly from 3toed dinosaurs.\nLIPE\nUTAH\nELEVATED\nSwe\n\"Mount\nmillo\nTHIS\nUT\nAPE\nKANAB\nMEXICALLY UNSPOILED\nUNIDOS\nN\n20421\nToms\nKANAB','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Dinosaur Tracks Hike\",\n  \"distance\": \"3.4 mi\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year Round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Steep and Rocky\",\n  \"elevation\": \"5700 feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.190355\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-112.645567\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"Car\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": false,\n  \"reservation_required\": false,\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": 2,\n  \"vehicle\": \"Kanab\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, travel north on Hwy 89, approximately 3 miles. Between milepost 67 and 68, turn right at the Port of Entry. Park at the south end by the porta-potty. Walk south through the sagebrush until you come to an old dirt road and head east. Follow that for a short distance until you see an opening in the rocks that will take you up to the next level. You will see a faint trail that follows the ledge to the right. After a short distance, the trail will meander up the hill until you reach the top.\",\n  \"description\": \"This hike takes you to an area where over 20 species of dinosaurs have been discovered. The highlights are the well-preserved dinosaur tracks from various types of animals, including a six-foot-long carnivorous dinosaur. The trail is steep and rocky, so watch your step. Rattlesnakes have been seen in the area as well.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>This hike takes you to an area where over 20 species of dinosaurs have been discovered. The highlights are the well-preserved dinosaur tracks from various types of animals, including a six-foot-long carnivorous dinosaur. The trail is steep and rocky, so watch your step. Rattlesnakes have been seen in the area as well.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"Please note that this map and directions are intended for general reference only. Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads and avoid dirt roads when wet. Respect private property and leave artifacts and rock art undisturbed. Please pack extra water, food, sunscreen, and necessary supplies as cell phone service is limited. Enjoy your visit responsibly.\"\n}','2023-09-22 02:50:07','2023-09-22 02:55:20'),(12,'trails',267,'',NULL,'sal\nEAGLE SINK\nOHV\nKU\nEAGLE SINK\nEAGLE SINK\nOHV\nSar\nTEND\nKANAB 20.75 mi\nToge\naur\nSmin)\nSIZ\nse\nWash\nMAILE\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\n3\nBEST SEASON:\nVEHICLE\nHIKING:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nDIFFICULTY:\nELEVATION:\n51.\nUS 89\n61521\nYEAR ROUND\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 5 100 E\n2.81 mi\n730\nGETTING THERE:\n•\nFrom Kanab, drive 20.75 miles East on HWY 89.\nAfter mile post 43, turn right.\n• Cross the cattle guard and follow this road.\n• It will Y into another road and you will be heading west.\n• Go a shot distance and watch for c T road to the left road # 730.\n• Take the Troad and watch for another Y and the sign pointing to Eagle Sink right.\nSHORT DISTANCE\n3 HOURS\nDISCLAIMER:\n\"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always chack local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designatec roads. Dirt roads cre mpassable when wel. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water\nfood, sunscreen, and suppies Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the rock art. Leave no trace of your vist so\nothers may enjoy.\"\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nWA\nYES\nMODERATE\n5800 FEET\n4WD\nleist\n730\n.68 mi\nSuptial\nof\nEagle Sink is an amazing limestone\nsinkhole that can be seen from the highway.\nThis hole is approximately 160 ft. deep\nand is in an area with 360 views, that is\nworth the drive itself. Part of the area is\nfenced of for your safety. The rest of the\narea is open, don\'t go near the edge,\nthere are alot of loose rocks and wind\ngusts. You may hear a lot of birds\nchirping and flying in and out of the suk\nhole. It is ocated approximately 21\nmiles east o Konab. The road requires\n4 WD that con tum slippery clay when\nwer. If you are feeling adventurous, there\nare other sink ho es in the area to explore.\nThis arventure is on the Grard Stair Case\nEscalare National Monument,\nܕܘܕܐ܀\nBOUT\nson\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILE\n6038\n4\'19\nEAGLE GPS 37 2.652\nSINK\n112 9 048\nHP\nTRAILS\nLANAB\nUT\nSAPP\nN\n2000\nThe\nLIFE\nADVENTURE MAP RESPECTE KANAB ABRA KANABRA! UTAH\nELEVATED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Eagle Sink\",\n  \"distance\": \"20.75 mi\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year Round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Moderate\",\n  \"elevation\": \"5800 feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.0442\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-112.1508\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"4WD\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Yes\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"3\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"4WD\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 20.75 miles East on HWY 89. After mile post 43, turn right. Cross the cattle guard and follow this road. It will Y into another road and you will be heading west. Go a short distance and watch for a T road to the left road #730. Take the T road and watch for another Y and the sign pointing to Eagle Sink right.\",\n  \"description\": \"Eagle Sink is an amazing limestone sinkhole that can be seen from the highway. This hole is approximately 160 ft. deep and is in an area with 360 views, that is worth the drive itself. Part of the area is fenced off for your safety. The rest of the area is open, don\'t go near the edge, there are a lot of loose rocks and wind gusts. You may hear a lot of birds chirping and flying in and out of the sinkhole. It is located approximately 21 miles east of Kanab. The road requires 4WD that can turn slippery clay when wet. If you are feeling adventurous, there are other sinkholes in the area to explore. This adventure is on the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>Eagle Sink is an amazing limestone sinkhole that can be seen from the highway. This hole is approximately 160 ft. deep and is in an area with 360 views, that is worth the drive itself. Part of the area is fenced off for your safety. The rest of the area is open, don\'t go near the edge, there are a lot of loose rocks and wind gusts. You may hear a lot of birds chirping and flying in and out of the sinkhole. It is located approximately 21 miles east of Kanab. The road requires 4WD that can turn slippery clay when wet. If you are feeling adventurous, there are other sinkholes in the area to explore. This adventure is on the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"Disclaimer: \\\"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads can be impassable when wet. Please respect Private Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas! Do not remove artifacts or deface the rock art. Leave no trace of your visit so others may enjoy.\\\", Phone: 435.644.5033\"\n}','2023-09-22 02:50:16','2023-09-22 02:55:47'),(13,'trails',268,'',NULL,'FUN FOR KIDS\nRECREATION,\nin\nFUN FOR KIDS\nRECREATION\nU.S. 89\nTO SALT LAKE,\nBRYCE & ZION\n<spart\n89\n300 North\n200 North\n100 North\nHORTH\n(89)\n9\n@上\nSOMBOR CITIZEN CENTER\nHOSPITAL\nw\nCENTER STREET\n100 South\nADVENTURE MAP\n200 South\n300 Scuth\nTENNIS:\nSWIMMING POOL:\nSKATE PARK:\nHIKING:\nBASKETBALL:\nPARK:\nKANAD TRAILS\nTRAILHEAD\nELEVATION:\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 5 100 E\nSQUAW TRAIL\nDADE\nGETTING THERE:\n•\nFrom the certer of town, at the stoolight at Center and Main, go north an Main Street,\nYou will see the library, tennis couts, basketball courts, pump track and baseball fields.\n• To get to the Jacob: Hamblin pork, tum right on 300 north.\n• Go I slack and turn left on 100 east.\nUBRARY\n* For Squaw trail drive past the park to the end of the road, to the trailhead.\n• Hike north and follow the signs\n€ 의\nYES\n• The trail goes to the lop, where you can connect to a whole trail system.\n• You may download the Kanab Treils Apo to help navigate trals.\nYES\nDISCLAIMER:\n\"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always chack local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designatec roads. Dirt roads cre mpassable when wel. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water\nfood, sunscreen, and suppies Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the rock art. Leave no trace of your vist so\nothers may enjoy.\"\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\n450 North\n405 North\nYES\n5771 FEET\n300 North\nYES\n200 North\n100 Nort\nYES\nYES\nLess than a mile from beautiful downtown\nKanab is Jacob Hanblin Fark Here you.\ncon cool off in the splash pad play\nvoleyball, basketball or teans and then\ntake a dip in the pool. There is also a\npumpTack for bikes and scooters\nAcove the baseball fields there is\narress in Squnw Tmil, a 3-mile indirip\nhike for the family. Kcnab has the best\npark ever. The pavillion can be reservered\nfor parties through Kanab City\'s website.\nDuring the summer, you may find the farmers\nMarket or movies in the park. We have the\nbest small town Independence Day\ncelebration in the country! Celebrate\nIndependence Day in the park, The Kanah\nWay. See the Kare County Vision Center for\ninformation and maps on our hiking trails.\n200 South\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nELEVATED\nKANE COUNTY VISITOR CENTER\n89\nLOCT\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILE\nU.S. 89\nWHAT\nCAPPE\nNORTH\nKANAB\nMasc. EPILE','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Fun for Kids Recreation\",\n  \"distance\": \"\",\n  \"best_season\": \"\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"\",\n  \"elevation\": \"\",\n  \"latitude\": \"\",\n  \"longitude\": \"\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From the center of town, at the stoplight at Center and Main, go north on Main Street. You will see the library, tennis courts, basketball courts, pump track, and baseball fields. To get to the Jacob Hamblin Park, turn right on 300 north and then go slack and turn left on 100 east. For the Squaw trail, drive past the park to the end of the road, to the trailhead. Hike north and follow the signs. The trail goes to the top, where you can connect to a whole trail system. You may download the Kanab Trails App to help navigate trails.\",\n  \"description\": \"Less than a mile from beautiful downtown Kanab is Jacob Hamblin Park. Here you can cool off in the splash pad, play volleyball, basketball, or tennis, and then take a dip in the pool. There is also a pump track for bikes and scooters. Above the baseball fields, there is access to Squaw Trail, a 3-mile family-friendly hike. Kanab has the best park ever. The pavilion can be reserved for parties through Kanab City\'s website. During the summer, you may find the farmers market or movies in the park. We have the best small-town Independence Day celebration in the country! Celebrate Independence Day in the park, The Kanab Way. See the Kane County Visitor Center for information and maps on our hiking trails.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>Less than a mile from beautiful downtown Kanab is Jacob Hamblin Park. Here you can cool off in the splash pad, play volleyball, basketball, or tennis, and then take a dip in the pool. There is also a pump track for bikes and scooters. Above the baseball fields, there is access to Squaw Trail, a 3-mile family-friendly hike. Kanab has the best park ever. The pavilion can be reserved for parties through Kanab City\'s website. During the summer, you may find the farmers market or movies in the park. We have the best small-town Independence Day celebration in the country! Celebrate Independence Day in the park, The Kanab Way. See the Kane County Visitor Center for information and maps on our hiking trails.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"Disclaimer: \\\"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads can be impassable when wet. Please respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas! Do not remove artifacts or deface the rock art. Leave no trace of your visit so others may enjoy.\\\" Phone: 435.644.5033\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:30:24','2023-09-22 02:31:59'),(14,'trails',269,'',NULL,'LOWER HACKBERRY CANYON\nSLOT CANYON HIKE\nCHIUS\nKANAB\n46.2 mi\nEAST\n89\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nADVENTURE MAP\nBEST SEASON: SPRING SUMMER FALL\nROAD:\nHIKING:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nDIFFICULTY:\nELEVATION:\nothers may enjoy.\"\nMAINTAINED DIRT ROAD\n4.5 MI ROUND TRIP\nHALF DAY\nEASY\nGETTING THERE:\n• From Kanab, drive 46.2 miles East on HWY 89.\n• Just post mile post 18, turn left onto road #200, the Cottonwood Road.\nLOWER HACKBERRY CANYON\nSLOT CANYON HIKE\n• Collonwood Road can be impossible when wet\n• Drive approximately 14.7 miles and turn turn left at the sign for the Lower Fackberry Canyon.\n• Park in the parking area, put on your water shoes and head west and drop into the wash.\n• Walk through the willows on the fo nt hiking path..\n• Soon you will enter Hackberry Canyon heading west.\nThe dinosaur tracts will be at 2.1 mile, on the left bank. Then hike back.\n• Water can be murky from sediment, conditions can include quicksand and muc slicks and can\nLe Fozarcous.\n• Be aware of possible flash floods during monsoon seaso1..\nDISCLAIMER:\n\"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designatec roads. Dirt roads cre mpassable when wel. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water.\nfood, sunscreen, and suppies Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the rock art. Leave no trace of your vist so\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 5 100 E\nLOWER\nHACKBERRY\nCANYON\n46.2 mi\nYES\n4770 FEET\n2.1 mi\nP\n14.7 mi\ntread lightly!\nSouthern Lich is famous for its slot canyons\nLower Hackberry Canyon is a beautiful\nnarrow slot canyon with clear ankle-deep\nwaler in a sandy stream bed. This is a great\nhike in the spring and early summer when\nit\'s hal, as the shalaw stream will keep\nyou cool. During the summer months\nthe stream may disappear. Families will\nenjoy this hike that offers a true wilderness\nexperience. A 2.1 miles in herolds dinosaur\ntracks on the left bank just above the wash\nlear. Sel on a wide bench with boulders,\nthere is also a 3 ft.x 5 ft. 1 rock with\ndistinct dinosaur racks. One can continue\nto hike up Hackberry Canyon to Sam\nPollock Arch or um cround here and go\nback as it is the most scoric.\n400\nCOTTONWOOD\nBegle\nLIFE\nLITAH\nELEVATED\nCANYON\nMILE\n1\n8\nMAN TEM\nur\nAPP\nB\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILE\nN\nKANAB\nMac ESPEILED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Lower Hackberry Canyon\",\n  \"distance\": \"4.5 mi\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Spring, Summer, Fall\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n  \"elevation\": \"4770 feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"\",\n  \"longitude\": \"\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"Yes\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Unknown\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"Half day\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"Car\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 46.2 miles East on HWY 89. Just past mile post 18, turn left onto road #200, the Cottonwood Road. Drive approximately 14.7 miles and turn left at the sign for Lower Hackberry Canyon. Park in the parking area, put on your water shoes and head west and drop into the wash. Walk through the willows on the front hiking path. Soon you will enter Hackberry Canyon heading west. The dinosaur tracks will be at 2.1 miles, on the left bank. Then hike back. Be aware of possible flash floods during monsoon season.\",\n  \"description\": \"Lower Hackberry Canyon is a beautiful narrow slot canyon with clear ankle-deep water in a sandy stream bed. This is a great hike in the spring and early summer when it\'s hot, as the shallow stream will keep you cool. During the summer months, the stream may disappear. Families will enjoy this hike that offers a true wilderness experience. At 2.1 miles in, there are dinosaur tracks on the left bank just above the wash. Set on a wide bench with boulders, there is also a 3 ft. x 5 ft. rock with distinct dinosaur tracks. One can continue to hike up Hackberry Canyon to Sam Pollock Arch or turn around here and go back as it is the most scenic.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>Lower Hackberry Canyon is a beautiful narrow slot canyon with clear ankle-deep water in a sandy stream bed. This is a great hike in the spring and early summer when it\'s hot, as the shallow stream will keep you cool. During the summer months, the stream may disappear. Families will enjoy this hike that offers a true wilderness experience. At 2.1 miles in, there are dinosaur tracks on the left bank just above the wash. Set on a wide bench with boulders, there is also a 3 ft. x 5 ft. rock with distinct dinosaur tracks. One can continue to hike up Hackberry Canyon to Sam Pollock Arch or turn around here and go back as it is the most scenic.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads can be impassable when wet. Please respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas! Do not remove 