e imposable when wat Macam impact\nPrivate Property. Be proposed for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra waing\nfood, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is son-arèstent în moal areas\nDo not remove ariffects or deface the \'rack arf. Laave no Iruns of your visit an\nother may enjoy.\nPHONE: 435.644.503.3\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nELEVATER\nRATE TRAINE\nUT\nAPP\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILED\nHI NIMI VATIED\nBeaut\nN\nKAT\nKANAB\nMEDICALLY ESPOILED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Sand Dune Arch\",\n  \"distance\": \"8 mi\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year Round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n  \"elevation\": \"6000 FEET\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.146567\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-112.585867\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"4WD\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"YES\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"NO\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"2 HOURS\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"NORTH\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 8 miles North on HWY 89. Just past milepost 71, turn left onto the Hancock Road. Drive approximately 8 miles and turn right on Road #648. Hike from here. At the point 4WD is required. Head towards a large hoodoo. Park here. Check out the view.\",\n  \"description\": \"If you are going to visit the Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park, or just have a few hours to spare, you will want to check out this unique arch in the sand located off of the Hancock Road. The arch is nestled among other beautiful rock formations, pinion and juniper trees.  You can hike from the Hancock Road or if you have 4WD, you can drive right up to it.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>If you are going to visit the Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park, or just have a few hours to spare, you will want to check out this unique arch in the sand located off of the Hancock Road. The arch is nestled among other beautiful rock formations, pinion and juniper trees. You can hike from the Hancock Road or if you have 4WD, you can drive right up to it.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"You will also see amazing views of the White Cliffs, one of the layers of the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads may be impassable when wet. Respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is limited in most areas. Do not remove artifacts or deface the rock art. Leave no traces of your visit so that others may enjoy.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:36:35','2023-09-22 02:43:19'),(46,'trails',301,'',NULL,'SMOKY MOUNTAINS\nSMOKE HOLES\n327\nNIPPLE\nBIG\nWATER\nBIG\nWATER\nVISITOR\nCENTER\n(KANAB 57 mi\n320\nROAD\n9.3 mi\nADVENTURE MAP\nA+ 000\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBEST SEASON:\nSMOKY\nVEHICLE:\nHIKING:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nSMOKY MOUNTAIN\nHIGH CLEARANCE 4WD\nDIFFICULTY:\nELEVATION:\nDISCLAIMER:\nothers may enjoy.\"\nTIBBITTS\nYEAR ROUND\nHIGH CLEARANCE 4WD\nGETTING THERE:\n• From Kanch, drive $7 miles east on HWY 89.\nJust past mile pust 7, turn left onto road #300, the Smoky Mountain Road.\n• Drive 9.3 miles to the intersection of Smoky Mountain Road and Crosby Canyon and stay left.\nDrive 3.7 miles to rood #332.\n• Continue for 9.9 miles and turn right, you will arrive at the smoke holes in less than a mic\negne, you\n• Dirt roads can be impesseble when wet. Check local weather conditions.\nCANYON\n* Near Big Water, the trail runs across the steep Kelly Grode, an exciting drive with narrow switchbacks.\nA few sections require a high clearance 4-wheel drive vehicle. This is a very remote desert, with limited\nto NO cell phone service. Be prepared, have a vehicle in good working order and bring plenty of\nfood water gas and a shovel.\n326\nYES\nSHORT DISTANCE\nROAD\nThis map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check locul oud and\nweather conditions. Stay on designatec roads. Dirt roads ore mpassable when wet. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water.\nfood, sunscreen, and suppies Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deloce the \'rock art. Leave no trace of your vist so\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 5 100 E\nFULL DAY\n300\nDIFFICULT\n4500-6500 FEET\n300 MOUNTAIN\nROAD\nThe Smoky Mountain road is the langes\nof the backcountry byways that cross\nthrough the Grand Staircase-Escalante\nNational Monnument. It got its name from\nan actual smaking mountain. Fire is burning\nin this scenic landscape and smake hos\nbeen seeping on this oncient\nunderground fire for millions of years.\nBecausn the ground is rich in ron deposits\nand most likely ignited by lightning, enough\noxygen enters surface cracks to keep the\ncoal smoldering. The distant blue of the\nlake is backdrop to a slark and barren\nlandscape here. Rolling mud hills of grey,\nbrows, gold and white host little\nvegetalion but a multitude of colors.\nAlong the route there are some beautiful\nviews of lake Powel.\nB\n3.7 mi.\nCROSBY CANYON\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nCLEVATED\n9.9 mi\nGPS 111 25.732\n37 13.601\nKANAB\nMASICALLY UNSPOILED\nHOLE\nಪ\n332\n77 mi\n340\nRETR\nUT KANAB\nAPP\nMILLY EXPL\nTorin','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Smoky Mountains\",\n  \"distance\": 327,\n  \"best_season\": \"Year Round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Difficult\",\n  \"elevation\": \"4500-6500 Feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.227\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-111.429\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"High Clearance 4WD\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Yes\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"Full Day\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"High Clearance 4WD\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 57 miles east on HWY 89. Just past mile post 7, turn left onto road #300, the Smoky Mountain Road. Drive 9.3 miles to the intersection of Smoky Mountain Road and Crosby Canyon and stay left. Drive 3.7 miles to road #332. Continue for 9.9 miles and turn right, you will arrive at the smoke holes in less than a mile.\",\n  \"description\": \"The Smoky Mountain Road is one of the backcountry byways that cross through the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. It got its name from an actual smoking mountain. Fire has been burning in this scenic landscape and smoke has been seeping from this ancient underground fire for millions of years. The ground is rich in iron deposits and most likely ignited by lightning, enough oxygen enters surface cracks to keep the coal smoldering. The distant blue of the lake is a backdrop to a stark and barren landscape here. Rolling mud hills of grey, brown, gold, and white host little vegetation but a multitude of colors. Along the route, there are some beautiful views of Lake Powell.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>The Smoky Mountain Road is one of the backcountry byways that cross through the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. It got its name from an actual smoking mountain. Fire has been burning in this scenic landscape and smoke has been seeping from this ancient underground fire for millions of years. The ground is rich in iron deposits and most likely ignited by lightning, enough oxygen enters surface cracks to keep the coal smoldering. The distant blue of the lake is a backdrop to a stark and barren landscape here. Rolling mud hills of grey, brown, gold, and white host little vegetation but a multitude of colors. Along the route, there are some beautiful views of Lake Powell.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"Near Big Water, the trail runs across the steep Kelly Grade, an exciting drive with narrow switchbacks. A few sections require a high clearance 4-wheel drive vehicle. This is a very remote desert, with limited to no cell phone service. Be prepared, have a vehicle in good working order and bring plenty of food, water, gas, and a shovel.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:36:47','2023-09-22 02:43:42'),(47,'trails',302,'',NULL,'SOUTH FORK\nINDIAN CANYON\nPICTOGRAPHS\nN\n#7\nYELLOW\nROAD\nJACKET\nSAND\nSPRING\n3.44 mi\nCORAL PINK SAND DUNES STATE PARK\n50\n