e sand, then gradually descend\nbeneath\n6200 FEET through roda Follow the\nHAN COCK ROAD\nthe diffband be your level you reach the\nalcore. \"Vw the wings behind the fence.\"\nLIFE\nUTAH\nELEVATED\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY DOSPOILED\nMC\n食\nMILE\n7.8 mi 7\nNORTH\n89\nPama?\nGENERATE\nUT KANAB\nMILLY PED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"South Fork Indian Canyon Pictographs\",\n  \"distance\": \"2 mi\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year Round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n  \"elevation\": \"6200 FEET\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.5683\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-112.5876\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"4WD/ATV\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Yes\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"1.5\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"4WD\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, take Highway 89 north for 7.2 miles and turn left after milepost 71 onto the Hancock Road. After 5.9 miles, turn left onto dirt road 50. AWD is now required, as the sand is very deep. Watch for an intersection with a sign directing you to turn east to the South Fork Indian Canyon Pictograph trailhead. The road narrows for the last 1.8 miles. The road ends at the point on the ridge. Start the hike on the well-beaten trail behind the Information sign.\",\n  \"description\": \"The Balmer people painted on these walls between 452 AD and 62 BC. The pictographs were painted with natural pigments found in this area like ochre, rabbit brush, and mineral deposits. Because of the delicate nature of ancient painting, they are usually found in sheltered alcoves where they have been protected from the elements. The Basketmaker people lived above these canyons in pithouses. They were farmers, hunters, and gatherers. The canyon below offered an abundant supply of water.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>The Balmer people painted on these walls between 452 AD and 62 BC. The pictographs were painted with natural pigments found in this area like ochre, rabbit brush, and mineral deposits. Because of the delicate nature of ancient painting, they are usually found in sheltered alcoves where they have been protected from the elements. The Basketmaker people lived above these canyons in pithouses. They were farmers, hunters, and gatherers. The canyon below offered an abundant supply of water.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local maps and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads are impassable when wet. Please respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in inaccessible areas. Do not remove or deface rock art. Leave no trace of your visit so others may enjoy.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:36:53','2023-09-22 02:44:01'),(48,'trails',303,'',NULL,'STAY A LITTLE LONGER\nALTERNATIVES TO THE WAVE\nBASECAMP KANAB\nWHITE POCKET\n4WD/ATV: difficult getting there\nHIKE: easy, 1 mi\nWIRE PASS\nHIKE: 2 mi, easy, one obstacle. 8ft. drop\nBUCKSKIN GUICH\nHIKE: difficult, 21 mi\nCEDAR MOUNTAIN\n-112 47.133, 37 32.637\n58 2 miles from Kanab\nALTERNATIVES\nTO THE WAVE\nPEEKABOO TRAIL\n-112 33.564, 37 10.774\n12.5 miles from Kanab\nCORAL PINK SAND DUNES\n-112 43.919, 37 2.263\n26 miles from Kanab\nHWY9\nHWY14\nINCHWORM ARCH\n4WD/ATV: easy but sandy\nHIKE: easy, .25 mi\nPEEK A BOO SLOT CANYON\n4WD: hard, sandy/ATV: moderate\nHKL: easy, I mi\nSo you\'ve come to Kanab to try your luck? It\'s a good thing your not in Las Vegas, because here you have nothing to losel Even if you\ndon\'- win the Wave lottery, you\'re clready lucky to be in the heart of the greatest concentration of natural woncers possibly anywhere in\nthe world. Every morning that you don\'t win the Wave lotery, it gives you a chance to explore a nearby national park or hidden gem.\nFor more information on alternative destinations, swing by the Kane County Visitor Center 78\'S 100 E Kanab UT.\nHWY89\nTOADSTOCI\nLIKE: easy family friendly.\n2 mi round trip\nCEDAR MOUNTA