thing to lose. Even if you don\'t win the Wave lottery, you\'re already lucky to be in the heart of the greatest concentration of natural wonders possibly anywhere in the world. Every morning that you don\'t win the Wave lottery, it gives you a chance to explore a nearby national park or hidden gem.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"For more information on alternative destinations, swing by the Kane County Visitor Center 78\'S 100 E Kanab UT.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:02','2023-09-22 02:44:13'),(49,'trails',304,'',NULL,'SOUAW\nPLANTS AND THEIR USES\nCANAR TRAILS\nUT\nAPP\nBehumantong sama manu UN\nTAURIMIDIFI\nSquaw Trail introduces you to the history, scenery and environment of Kancb, Utah. Kanab is a Paiute word for \"Place of the Willows\". The\nnumbered post along the trail correspond to the text on this fyer. For thousands of years the Anasazi Indians, Paiutes, pioneer children, and\nour children have enjoyed scrambling up and down this trail. Indians and settlers found many uses for the plants here. Listed below are a few.\nThis hike can be taxing in the heat of the day. Please take advantage of shade and bring plenty of water. Enjoy the beauly around you.\nKanab Visitor\'s Contor: 78 S 100 E\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nTRAILHEAD\nPARKING\n1. SALT BRUSH\nNavajo indians used this plants leaves and\ntwigs to produce yellow paint.\n4. ASTER\nThe leaves were boiled and used as greens\nby the ureus\'s earliest inhabitants.\ntread lightly!\n7. RAVENS\nYou will often see ravens circling the sky and\nyou may hear Canyon Wrens. It is possible\nto dentify 75 species of birds along the trail.\n10. FINYON PINE\nPinenuts were a staple food for the Indians.\n13. UTAH JUNIPER\nThe indians used the berries as beads in\nnecklaces. The berries are also edible.\n16. KAYENTA FORMATION\nTake a look at the outcrooping of the Kayoma\nFormation. The ripple and scour marks\nindicate movement of water cumarts that\nexisted over 170 million years ago.\n19. JTAH JUNIPER\nIs clsc called Juniper Mistletoe or Wilchas\nBroom.\nOVERLOOK\nOVERLOOK 2\n16\n2. THE VERMILION CLIFFS\n3 geologic formations. The Moenove,\nKayento, and the lower part of the Navajo\nSandstone.\n5. MOENAVE FORMATION\nWas the lower part of the Vermilion Cliffs that\nwas deposited over 180 million years ago.\nLIFE\nUTAH\nELEVATCO\n8. MORMON TEA\nBrewed by the Indians and later the Mormons\ninto an aromatic lea. Used for the treatment\nof coughs, colds, headoche and faver\nUSKILA\n11. ROCK FORMATION\nforces of weather and erosion, such as water\nfreezing or thawing, running water are active\nupon the rock cliffs.\n14. UTAH SERV CEBERRY\nThe berries in the fall were a favorite of the\nIndians who are them fresh.\nDAN\n17. SAGE BRUSH\nThe best known plant in the southwest. has\na refreshing, pugrat xdor. Seeds were eaten\nraw. It has been used for the treatment of\ncolds and as an antiseptic. It burns with on\nin lense heat and was a major fuel source for\nthe Indians and settlers.\n20. ANTILOPE BITTERBRUSII\nle a favorite of mule deer, you will often find\ndeer droppings near this plant\nOVERLOOK 3\n18\n3. COLDEN RABBIT BRUSH\nThe fex ble branches of the Rabbitkrush were\nused by the Hopi for basket making\n6. CUFFROSE\nThe leaves and twigs were used as a cleansing\nand healing agent flor sores and wounds. The\nbork was shreaced and braided to make\ndothing, sandals, mots and rope.\nTWAS\nUT,\nAPP\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILED\n9. BEAUTIFUL SCENERY\nKanab was the sering for the filming of over\n60 movies and mony TV series such as the\nLone Ranger.\n12. WILDLIFE\nOr warm sunny days, various species of birds.\nlizards and snakes including rettlesnakes con\nbe seen on this mail.\n15. INDIAN PAINTBRUSH\nOne of the most colorful wildflowers in the\nsouthwest. Its blooms were often acten by\nthe indians.\n18. CRAMBEL OAK\nThe acons of the shrubby Gamble Ook were\nhighly przed by the Indians. The acorns were\nground Into meal to make mush, soup, bread\nand pancakes.\n21. YUCCA\nThe sharp-pointed leaves of the Yucca\nprovided the Indians with needles and thread.\nif you break the tip and pull down you will\nobtain and attached thin fiber of thread.\nYoung stalk may be eaten raw or roasted.\n22. GRAND STAIRCASE\nHave a seat on the bench and enjoy the view of the Grend Staircase. It is formed by a series of five diffs, or tarace. The five steps are differentiated by\ntheir color Kanale lies crop the first step, the Chocolate Cliffs. The Squaw Iria is located in the middle of the second step, the Vermilion Cliffs from tha\ntop of the trail you will be able to look north and see the rest of the steps. The White Cliffs (Zion National Park). The Groy Ciffs (Aton areal. The Pink\nCliffs, the top slep (Bryce Canyoni. We hope you enjoyed this hike and that is has helped you appreciate the world around you. At this point you can\nhead back the same way you came or you can continue to explore the area.','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Squaw Trail\",\n  \"distance\": \"\",\n  \"best_season\": \"\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"\",\n  \"elevation\": \"\",\n  \"latitude\": \"\",\n  \"longitude\": \"\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"Squaw Trail introduces you to the history, scenery, and environment of Kanab, Utah. The trail is known for its beautiful views and is a popular spot for hiking. Along the trail, you will find numbered posts that correspond to information about the plants and geology of the area. The trail is moderately challenging, especially in the heat, so it is recommended to take breaks and bring plenty of water. The Kanab Visitor\'s Center is the starting point of the trail.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>Squaw Trail introduces you to the history, scenery, and environment of Kanab, Utah. The trail is known for its beautiful views and is a popular spot for hiking. Along the trail, you will find numbered posts that correspond to information about the plants and geology of the area. The trail is moderately challenging, especially in the heat, so it is recommended to take breaks and bring plenty of water. The Kanab Visitor\'s Center is the starting point of the trail.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"For more information, contact the Kanab Visitor\'s Center at 435.644.5033.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:11','2023-09-22 02:44:26'),(50,'trails',305,'',NULL,'SQUAW TRAIL\nBILE\nSOUAW TRAIL\nHIKE\n5007\nDry\nPARK\nPARRY\nLODGE\nKANAB VISITOR CENTER\n▬▬▬\nADVENTURE MAP\n88 O\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBEST SEASON:\nVEHICLE:\nHIKING:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nDIFFICULTY:\nELEVATION:\nother may enjoy.\nCanyon\nYEAR ROUND\nSQUAW\nTRAIL\n3 MILES ROUND TRIP\n3039\nNO\nGETTING THERE\nAt the coplight of Cemer and ivain, tales a right onto Main Street,\n* Tem right on 300 north,\n2-3 HRS\nMODERATE\n* Go 1 block and tem left on 100 E\nDrive port Jacob Hamblin Park on the left and continue straight to the mathead.\nStart at the Squaw Thal Sign.\n• Head north and follow the signe.\nTrader\nANY\n5040-5802 FEET\nThe trail goes to the top where you can connect to a whole trall system.\nSee the Kane County Vietor Center for additional maps.\nYou may also download the Kanab Trails App.\nDISCLAIMER:\nThis map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roods are imposable when wel. Maca impact\nPrivate Property, Be prepared for a wildernos experience: Always bring sama wote,\nfood, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is son-arèstent în moal areas\nDo not remove ariffects or deface the \'rack orf. Laave no Irons of your visit an\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nColly Vister\'s Corner: 705 100 E\nDry\nSpring\nHOT\nms.\nSavage\nPoint\nKanabis becoming wall know for all\nof thiking trails, Squaw Trailis favorite\nas it can be accessed right from town.\nIf you only do one hile while vising\nKanab, kthis onl\nThe trail is considered acryl\nmoderate, being somewhat step in\narea with 780 ft. elevation\nThere are spectacular views of the\ntown, Arizona and auneunding\nareas. The trail has many anxing\nrock formations and when you reach\nthe top, you can connect to a whole\nnetwork of other trails\nWHIKEKANAB\nermilion\n59p\nΚΑΝΑΒ\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nELEVATED\nTAH\nCliffs.\n5800\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILED\nPurgh gain\nCARTEAKS\nAPP\nN\nToms\n5800\nموها\nKANAB\nHOLLY SPOILED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Squaw Trail\",\n  \"distance\": \"3 miles round trip\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Moderate\",\n  \"elevation\": \"5040-5802 feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"\",\n  \"longitude\": \"\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"No\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Unknown\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"2-3 hours\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"Any\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"At the coplight of Cemer and ivain, take a right onto Main Street, then right on 300 north. Go 1 block and turn left on 100 E. Drive past Jacob Hamblin Park on the left and continue straight to the trailhead. Start at the Squaw Trail sign. Head north and follow the signs. See the Kane County Visitor Center for additional maps. You may also download the Kanab Trails App.\",\n  \"description\": \"Kanab is becoming well known for its hiking trails, and Squaw Trail is a favorite as it can be accessed right from town. If you only do one hike while visiting Kanab, make it this one. The trail is considered a moderately steep area with a 780 ft. elevation. There are spectacular views of the town, Arizona, and the surrounding areas. The trail has many amazing rock formations, and when you reach the top, you can connect to a whole network of other trails.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>Kanab is becoming well known for its hiking trails, and Squaw Trail is a favorite as it can be accessed right from town. If you only do one hike while visiting Kanab, make it this one. The trail is considered a moderately steep area with a 780 ft. elevation. There are spectacular views of the town, Arizona, and the surrounding areas. The trail has many amazing rock formations, and when you reach the top, you can connect to a whole network of other trails.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"DISCLAIMER: This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads may become impassable when wet. Respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service may be inconsistent in most areas. Do not remove artifacts or deface the trail. Leave no traces of your visit.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:17','2023-09-22 02:44:46'),(51,'trails',306,'',NULL,'LOTTERY LOCATION\nThe only legal lottery in Utah\nGRIND\n89\nFLORY\nLOBAR\nWAVE LOTTERY\nORIENTATION VISITOR\nCENTER\n76 S\n1\nTOO E\n0\n0\nKANAB\nCONVENTION\nCENTER\nNORTH COYOTE BUTTES\n(The Wave)\npick up permits 6- 8:30 am\nsafety briefing 9:00 am\n89A\n• Due to the fragile nature of the area a permit is required for hiking South Coyote Buttes\nand North Coyote Buttes \"The Wave\".\nSOUTH COYOTE BUTTES\npickup permit 9:00 am\nsafety briefing 9:15 am\n• North Coyote Buttes walk-in is limited to 4 groups or 16 people which ever is drawn first.\n• South Coyote Buttes is 10 permits a day\n• Permits are drawn by online lottery at Rec.gov\n• Pick up permits at Kane County Office of Tourism /8 S. 100 E.\nKANAB\nMARICALLY UNSPOILED\n...\nPROCESS:\nDay 1: Apply at 6 am to 6pm. You will be notified by email that\'s sent out at 7:15 pm. Accept by 8 am.\nDay 2: Complete acceptance by 8 am, pick up at 8 am (Tourism Office) and 8:30 am safety briefing.\nDay 3: Hike the North or South Coyote Buttes whichever permit you won.\nVisitSouthernUtah.com 435 644-5033','{\n\"trail_name\": \"Lottery Location\",\n\"distance\": \"\",\n\"best_season\": \"\",\n\"difficulty\": \"\",\n\"elevation\": \"\",\n\"lati