 gets its name\nfrom the toadstool formations\nas seen at the end of this hike. At\ntoadstool is a rock formation which\nhas a boulder perched on a rock of\na smaller diameter, like a mushroom,\nor \"toadstool.\" This environment is the\nperfect condition for these formations.\n2 MILES ROUND TRIP\nGPS 376.494\n112 52.254\nTOADSTOOLS\n1 mi\n4456\nFULL SUN\nGETTING THERE:\n• From Kanab drive 45 miles east on HWY 89\n• The parking lot and trailhead will be just past milepost 20 on your left.\n• The trail begins just behind the information sign.\n• Follow the wash and the well defined trail to the noodoos.\n.\n• Exploring the surrounding area, you will see many balancing rock and hoodoos.\n• This environment is an open and shaceless desert. It is extremely hot in the summer.\n• Be prepared with enough water and sunscreen.\nEAST\n89\nNO\nHALF DAY\nDISCLAIMER:\n\"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dit roads are impassable when wet. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water,\nfood, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-ex stant in most araasl\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the \'rock art\'. Leave no trace of your visit so\nothers may enjoy\nKanal Visitor\'s Center: 78 S 100 E\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\n4456 FEET\nHE\n4655\n4302\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nELEVATED\n3.6.\nHAL TRANS\nAPP\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY POILED\nMR\nRIM\nTRAILHEAD\n220\nKANAB\nNega LED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Toadstools Hike\",\n  \"distance\": \"2 miles round trip\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy Family Friendly\",\n  \"elevation\": \"600 feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"37.6494\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-112.8709\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"Full sun\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"No\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"Half day\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"Car\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 45 miles east on HWY 89. The parking lot and trailhead will be just past milepost 20 on your left. The trail begins just behind the information sign. Follow the wash and the well-defined trail to the hoodoos.\",\n  \"description\": \"Have you ever wanted to visit another planet? Well, this is the hike for you! Bring your camera to take stunning pictures of the alluring natural wonders of balancing rocks and hoodoos with their unique colors and shapes. The toadstool formations at the end of the hike are rock formations with boulders perched on smaller rocks, resembling mushrooms or \'toadstools\'. This hike is family-friendly and offers an otherworldly experience.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>Have you ever wanted to visit another planet? Well, this is the hike for you! Bring your camera to take stunning pictures of the alluring natural wonders of balancing rocks and hoodoos with their unique colors and shapes. The toadstool formations at the end of the hike are rock formations with boulders perched on smaller rocks, resembling mushrooms or \'toadstools\'. This hike is family-friendly and offers an otherworldly experience.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"Exploring the surrounding area, you will see many balancing rocks and hoodoos. This environment is an open and shadeless desert, extremely hot in the summer. Be prepared with enough water and sunscreen. Please check local road and weather conditions before visiting. Respect private property and do not remove artifacts or deface rock art. Leave no trace of your visit for others to enjoy.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:38','2023-09-22 02:45:18'),(53,'trails',308,'',NULL,'89\nSCENIC\nKANAB\n>60 mi\nADVENTURE MAP\nMAX\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBYWAY 12\nBEST SEASON:\nDIFFICULTY:\nELEVATION:\nGETTING THERE:\nTROPIC RESERVOIR\nVEHICLE:\nLOTS OF ATV TRAILS\nHIKING: LOTS OF HIKING OPPORTUNITIES\nACCOMMODATIONS:\n19 CAMP SITES\nothers may enjoy.\"\n• Fram Kanab drive 60 miles north on Scenic Highway 89.\nTum right on Scenic Byway 12. Slow down and enjoy the beauty of Red Canyonl\n• Trave about 13.5 miles, and just past mile post 10 turn right (road #87).\nAfter 7 miles you will see a sign for Tropic Reservoir unc Kings Creek Campground.\n• Tum right and continue around the reservoir for a half a mile to the parking area for the\nPansaugunt trailhead.\n• If you have time take the 3 mile AIV trail to Ruby\'s Inn for breakfast lunch or dinner.\n12\nDISCLAIMER:\nThis map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designatec roads. Dirt roads cre mpassable when wel. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water\nfood, sunscreen, and suppies Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the \'rock art. Leave no trace of your vist so\nKANAB\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILE\nPALINJAUCUNT\nRED CANYON\nTROPIC RESERVOIR\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 5 100 E\nDIXIE NATIONAL FOREST\nTropic Reservoir is a small scanic lake,\nsituated in the Dixie National Forest of\nSouthern Utah. It is very popular lor fishing,\nboating, ATV riding, mountain biking, hiking.\nand snowmobiling. The Pounsagaunt Trail\nhus views of the \'Fink Cliffs on the\nGrand Staircase National Forest\nand Keibah Plamau. Kings Cmak\nCampground has a paved boct ramp,\nsurable for small bacts and kayaks. Fishing\nfor brown, brook, rainbow and cuthreat\ntrou is great from the shore or boo. An\nATV ride west from the compground und\nover the beautiful mountain will get you to\nover the\nHatch. Riding the trails south and post will\ntake you to spectacular orange hoodoos\nEASY\n7835 FEET just outsice Bryce Canyon, with breathtaking\nscenery and excellert hiking opportunities.\n18 ml\nMAY-SEPTEMBER\nATV LOOP\nNO\n13.5 mi\nMILE\nEAST\nX\nTROPIC\nRESERVOIR\n7 mi\nISTE\n# 87\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nELEVATED\n2.8 mi\n(12)\nAT TRAIL TO RUBY\'S INN\nBRYCE\nCANYON\nUN N\nUT\nAPP\n2.6 mi\nRUBY\'S INN\nNATIONAL\nPARK\nN\nKANAB\nMISPRIED','{\n\"trail_name\": \"Scenic Kanab\",\n\"distance\": \">60 mi\",\n\"best_season\": \"May-September\",\n\"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n\"elevation\": \"7835 feet\",\n\"latitude\": null,\n\"longitude\": null,\n\"accessibility\": \"No\",\n\"pets_allowed\": null,\n\"reservation_required\": null,\n\"average_hours_to_hike\": null,\n\"vehicle\": \"ATV\",\n\"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 60 miles north on Scenic Highway 89. Turn right on Scenic Byway 12. Slow down and enjoy the beauty of Red Canyon. Travel about 13.5 miles and just past mile post 10 turn right (road #87). After 7 miles, you will see a sign for Tropic Reservoir and Kings Creek Campground. Turn right and continue around the reservoir for half a mile to the parking area for the Panguitch trailhead. If you have time, take the 3-mile ATV trail to Ruby\'s Inn for breakfast, lunch, or dinner.\",\n\"description\": \"Tropic Reservoir is a small scenic lake situated in the Dixie National Forest of Southern Utah. It is very popular for fishing, boating, ATV riding, mountain biking, hiking, and snowmobiling. The Panguitch Trail offers views of the Pink Cliffs on the Grand Staircase National Forest and Kaibab Plateau. Kings Creek Campground has a paved boat ramp suitable for small boats and kayaks. Fishing for brown, brook, rainbow, and cutthroat trout is great from the shore or boat. An ATV ride west from the campground and over the beautiful mountain will get you to Hatch. Riding the trails south and east will take you to spectacular orange hoodoos just outside Bryce Canyon, with breathtaking scenery and excellent hiking opportunities.\",\n\"description_html\": \"Tropic Reservoir is a small scenic lake situated in the Dixie National Forest of Southern Utah. It is very popular for fishing, boating, ATV riding, mountain biking, hiking, and snowmobiling. The Panguitch Trail offers views of the Pink Cliffs on the Grand Staircase National Forest and Kaibab Plateau. Kings Creek Campground has a paved boat ramp suitable for small boats and kayaks. Fishing for brown, brook, rainbow, and cutthroat trout is great from the shore or boat. An ATV ride west from the campground and over the beautiful mountain will get you to Hatch. Riding the trails south and east will take you to spectacular orange hoodoos just outside Bryce Canyon, with breathtaking scenery and excellent hiking opportunities.\",\n\"additional_info\": \"Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads can be impassable when wet. Please respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas. Please do not remove artifacts or deface rock art. Leave no trace of your visit. Phone: 435.644.5033\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:45','2023-09-22 02:45:42'),(54,'trails',309,'',NULL,'WAHWEAP HOODOOS\nHIKE\nD\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBEST SEASON:\nVEHICLE:\nKANAB 55 mi\nHIKING:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nWAHWEAP HOODOOS\nA DAY\nDIFFICULTY: DEPENDING ON WEATHER\nEASE TO HARD\nELEVATION:\nGETTING THERE\n• From Kanab, take HWY 89 east, approximately 55 miles to Big Water.\n• Turn left on the Ethan Allen Road, between mile 7 and 6, across the highway from the\nGrand Slaircuse Escalante Visitor Center.\n• Follow Ethan Allen past several streats to a \"T\" in the road. Turn left at the \"T\" and\nbypass the old softbell feld on the left side of the gravel rond. Follow this road pas two\nfish hatcheries.\n46\nlowers of Silence\n37 09.749 HOODOO\n111 42.708\nHOODOO\nYES\nSPRING FALL\nHIKE ONLY\n9 MILES ROUND TRIP\nContinue north past the corral which is 3 miles from HWY 89. 2 wheel drive vehicles,\npark on the right side of the road just before the creek.\n• Start your hike here by hiking up Wohweap Creek.\nHigh clearance 4-wheel drive vehicles can continue past the corral, crossing\nWahweap Creek, and ther continua 3 miles. The trail begins ir Wahweap Creek just\nbelow the 4WD parking.\n• Hike north up Wahweap Creek:\nDISCLAIMER:\nThis map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local roed and\nweather conditions, Stoy on designcted roads. Dirt roads are impassable when wet. Please respect\nPrivole Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water,\nfood, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in mos greas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the \'rock art\'. Lecve no trace of\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nyou visit so others may enjoy.\"\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 S 100 E\nCOYOTE CREEK\n102\nCX1\n(89)\nTrailhead 4WD parking PARK HERE WITH 4WD (P)\n3706.769\n111 41,309\n4042 FEET\nSLOT CANYONS\nREEK\nNIPPLE CREEK\nHoodoos and balancing rocks are a\nnatural wonder to see. Some describe\nthese as goblins, ghosts or toadstocals.\nEnjoy the unique geology of the sun\nscorchod lands of the Southwest. The\ncap of the hoodoo. a Dakota Sandstone\nthat was a beach of an incoming\nseaway, is 100 million years old.\nThe post is Entrade Sandstone that ist\n160 million years old. The headeas\ncome in many shapes and sizes and con\nbe reached by taking a leisurely, easily\nnovigaled hike through Wahweup Creek\nThere are a few little slot canyons along\nWahweap, Creek that you could also\nexplore. The Piutos believed that the\nhoodoos are the remnants of people\nwho were turned to pane. Caze up\ninto these cloves, in the norly morning\nand you will see why.\nP PARK HERE WITH 2WD\nNILE\nFISH HATCHERY\nO\nBIG WATER\nGSENMA\nVISITOR CENTER\nFISH HATCHERY\nGRAVEL PIT\nO\nLIFE\nADVENTURE MAP tread lightly! UTAH\n* O *\nCLEVATED\nGRAVEL PIT\nUT,\nAPP\nKANAB\nMODICILLUNSPOILED\nN\n1457\nKANAB\nKUGLY LOSPOLE','{\n   \"trail_name\": \"Wahweap Hoodoos\",\n   \"distance\": \"9 miles round trip\",\n   \"best_season\": \"Spring, Fall\",\n   \"difficulty\": \"Depends on weather, Easy to Hard\",\n   \"elevation\": \"4042 feet\",\n   \"latitude\": \"37.162485\",\n   \"longitude\": \"-111.711799\",\n   \"accessibility\": \"Yes\",\n   \"pets_allowed\": \"No\",\n   \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n   \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"A Day\",\n   \"vehicle\": \"2-wheel drive vehicles can park on the right side of the road just before the creek. High clearance 4-wheel drive vehicles can continue past the corral, crossing Wahweap Creek and park at the 4WD parking area just below the trailhead.\",\n   \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, take HWY 89 east for approximately 55 miles to Big Water. Turn left on the Ethan Allen Road between mile 7 and 6, across the highway from the Grand Staircase-Escalante Visitor Center. Follow Ethan Allen Road until you reach a T-junction and turn left. Continue past the corral for 3 miles. 2WD vehicles should park on the right side of the road. The trailhead starts by hiking up Wahweap Creek.\",\n   \"description\": \"Wahweap Hoodoos is a scenic hike located near Big Water, Utah. The trail takes hikers through Wahweap Creek, offering views of unique geological formations, including hoodoos and balancing rocks. The hike is approximately 9 miles round trip and can be completed in a day. The difficulty level of the hike varies depending on weather conditions, ranging from easy to hard. The best seasons to hike are spring and fall when the weather is mild. Pets are not allowed on the trail. It is recommended to bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies for a wilderness experience.\",\n   \"description_html\": \"<p>Wahweap Hoodoos is a scenic hike located near Big Water, Utah. The trail takes hikers through Wahweap Creek, offering views of unique geological formations, including hoodoos and balancing rocks. The hike is approximately 9 miles round trip and can be completed in a day. The difficulty level of the hike varies depending on weather conditions, ranging from easy to hard. The best seasons to hike are spring and fall when the weather is mild. Pets are not allowed on the trail. It is recommended to bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies for a wilderness experience.</p>\",\n   \"additional_info\": \"Always check local road and weather conditions before starting the hike. Stay on designated roads and avoid dirt roads when wet. Respect private property and do not remove artifacts or deface rock art. Leave no trace and carry out all trash. Cell phone service is limited in the area.\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:37:55','2023-09-22 02:46:05'),(55,'trails',310,'',NULL,'WARRIORS WALK\nATV/JEEP\nMAIL\nDROP\nKAMA SHUNES\nHOLLOW\n2\nWARRIORS WALK\n19A\n25\n19\nOFF THE BEATON PATH:\nKJ220\nBEST SEASON: ALL MOST YEAR ROUND\nVEHICLE:\nHIKING:\nAVERAGE TIME:\nDIFFICULTY:\nWARRIORS WALK\nATV/JEEP\n19\nELEVATION:\nGETTING THERE:\nothers may enjoy.\"\n17C\n• From Kanab, drive 7.8 miles north on HWY 89.\n• Just past mile post 71, turn left onto the Hancock road.\n• Drive 9.3 miles to the stop sign and turn left.\n• Drive 9.1 miles and turn right on road K1250 it is clso signed 13200 West.\n• Drive 2.2 miles to sign with road map. You can park here and unload ATV\'s.\n• The trail starts on your left. Drive 4.7 miles and turn left on road on 17.\n• Drive approximally 2 miles, stay right on road 19.\n• At about 2.5 miles turn lef on road 19A.\nThe road will end, park here. Look at hat view of Zion!!!\nK 1250\nDISCLAIMER:\n\"This map and directions are intended for general reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designatec roads. Dirt roads cre mpassable when wel. Please respect\nPrivate Property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water\nfood, sunscreen, and suppies Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the rock art. Leave no trace of your vist so\nPHONE: 435.644.5033\nK 1245\n4.49 ml\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 78 5 100 E\n25F\n10H\n17\nROUGH\nSECTION\n17B\n25\nSHORT DISTANCE\nK(1290\nK 1215\n10G\n10 25\nHALF DAY\nDIFFICULT\nLURRIAREN\nSAND BUTTE\n10\nADVENTURE MAP tread lightly!\nA810\n5771 FEET\nK 1287\n2.7 mi\nYES\n10\n4WD\n27\nP\n2.2 mi\nSPRING\n20M\nWarriors Walk is a populor OHV rice for\nKane County laccls. The ride starts 4 miles\nwest of the Coral Pink Sand Dures State Park\nand heads north post Elephant Butte. The ride.\nis mostly sendy truils winding through sage\nsush and jur pars, with a few sections of\nsandstona. Gelling here can be\nchallenging, Larger 4WD vehicles will\nhave a diff cult time getting to this location.\nWhen you arrive at Warriors Walk you will\nsce inspiring scenery and sandstone sickrock\nwith views of Zion in the background. It is\nuncertain who left this pathway across the\nsandstore. Could it be the Native American\nPeople who left a hail lined with rocks hrough\nthe deser? No ane is aluin Bring a cumeru,\nyou won\'t want to miss this photo opportunity.\nIf you have time you can also visit the Mail\nCrop or explore the many trails in the area.\n25D\nV\nK2150\n20\nELEPHANT BUTTE ELEPHANT GAP\nK 1260 20\nLIFE\nUTAH\nELEVATED\n20\n25\n20\n20\n[20]\nK1255 P\nKANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILE\nN\n62 SAND DUNES\nPATINE\nUT\nAPP\nELEPHANT COVE\nSTAGING AREA\nKANAB\nMG CALLE ESPOILED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"Warriors Walk\",\n  \"distance\": 19,\n  \"best_season\": \"All year round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Moderate\",\n  \"elevation\": 5771,\n  \"latitude\": 37.3289,\n  \"longitude\": -112.7177,\n  \"accessibility\": \"Yes\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Yes\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": 5,\n  \"vehicle\": \"ATV/JEEP\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 7.8 miles north on HWY 89. Just past mile post 71, turn left onto the Hancock road. Drive 9.3 miles to the stop sign and turn left. Drive 9.1 miles and turn right on road K1250 it is also signed 13200 West. Drive 2.2 miles to sign with road map. You can park here and unload ATV\'s. The trail starts on your left. Drive 4.7 miles and turn left on road 17. Drive approximately 2 miles, stay right on road 19. At about 2.5 miles, turn left on road 19A. The road will end, park here. Look at that view of Zion!!!\",\n  \"description\": \"Warriors Walk is a popular OHV ride for Kane County locals. The ride starts 4 miles west of the Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park and heads north past Elephant Butte. The ride is mostly sandy trails winding through sagebrush and juniper trees, with a few sections of sandstone. Getting here can be challenging. Larger 4WD vehicles will have a difficult time getting to this location. When you arrive at Warriors Walk, you will see inspiring scenery and sandstone slickrock with views of Zion in the background. It is uncertain who left this pathway across the sandstone. Could it be the Native American people who left a trail lined with rocks through the desert? No one knows for sure. Bring a camera, you won\'t want to miss this photo opportunity. If you have time, you can also visit the Mail Drop or explore the many trails in the area.\",\n  \"description_html\": \"<p>Warriors Walk is a popular OHV ride for Kane County locals. The ride starts 4 miles west of the Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park and heads north past Elephant Butte. The ride is mostly sandy trails winding through sagebrush and juniper trees, with a few sections of sandstone. Getting here can be challenging. Larger 4WD vehicles will have a difficult time getting to this location. When you arrive at Warriors Walk, you will see inspiring scenery and sandstone slickrock with views of Zion in the background. It is uncertain who left this pathway across the sandstone. Could it be the Native American people who left a trail lined with rocks through the desert? No one knows for sure. Bring a camera, you won\'t want to miss this photo opportunity. If you have time, you can also visit the Mail Drop or explore the many trails in the area.</p>\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"Always check local road and weather conditions. Stay on designated roads. Dirt roads may be impassable when wet. Please respect private property. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water, food, sunscreen, and supplies. Cell phone service is non-existent in most areas! Do not remove artifacts or deface the rock art. Leave no trace of your visit.\",\n  \"telephone\": \"435.644.5033\"\n}','2023-09-10 06:38:03','2023-09-22 02:46:33'),(56,'trails',311,'',NULL,'WHITE POCKET\nHIGH CLEARANCE 4WD\nWHITE POCKET\nHIGH CLEARANCE 4WD\nN\nKANAB 38.6 mi.\nMONT\nADVENTURE MAP\n(89\nOFF THE BEATEN PATH:\nBEST SEASON:\nCORRAL\nVEHICLE:\nHIKING:\n+\nNOTE: Much of White Pocket is\nprivately ranched. Please respect\nthe privacy of others. Please do not\ndisturb their property and animals.\nAVERAGE TIME:\nDIFFICULTY: EASY HIKE/DEEP SAND\nELEVATION:\n5673 FEET\n2013 ml.\n1079\nGETTING THERE:\n• From Karab drive 38.6 miles east on HWY 89.\n• Between milepost 26 and 25, you will see a dirt road that heads south.\n• This is House Rock Valley Road generally a very well maintained hard packed dirt road.\n• At 20.3 miles and turn left an road 1017, at the horse corral.\n• From this point, high clearance, 4WD vehicles are required. Park and unload your ATV here.\n• Let some air out of your tires to navigate the sandy terrain.\n• Travel 6.2 miles on 1017\n1017\n*\nAt the junction, cortinua straight on road 1087\n• Drive 3.9 miles and stay left on road 1036.\n• Continue on 1086 for 5.6 miles to The White Pocket trailhead.\n• Park at the trailhead and begin your short hike.\nDISCLAIMER:\n\"This map and directions are intended for genera reference use only. Always check local road and\nweather conditions. Stay on designated roads Dirt roads are impassable when wat. Please respect\nPrivate Properly. Be prepared for a wilderness experience: Always bring extra water,\nfood, sunscreen, and supples. Cell phone service is non-existent in most arcas!\nDo not remove artifacts or deface the \'rock ar\'. Leave no trace of your visit so\nothers may enjoy.\"\nKanab Visitor\'s Center: 785 100 E\nPHONE: 435.544.5033\nVALLEY\nPAW\nHOLE\nYEAR ROUND\nRED\nPOCKET\n1066\nYES\n4WD / ATV\nMILE\n2\n6\nALTERNATE ROUTE\n1 MILE\nFULL DAY\nCORRAL\nCOWBOY\n6.25 ml. CABIN\n1087\n11084 1084\n1064\n11 53.734\n36 37.328\nWHITE POCKET\nMany visitors come to Scuthern Utah\ndo The Wave one of the most photo-\ngraphed rack \'ormations in the world! Unlo\nJunately, due to its fragile nature, permits are\nImited to maximum 64 hikers a cay. Many\ndon\'t realize that \"The Wave is only the\nbeginning of a long list of stunning\nformations the area has to offer. Locals\npreler White Pocket, a much larger area\nthen The Wove. A moonscape like\nlandscase hat requires no permit! The\nswiring twisted, multi colored natural\nartwork of White Pocke, will fill your senses\nbeyond imagination. Get an early start, as\nthis is an all day adventure. A 4WD, high\nclearance vehicle is a must for this journey,\nor hire a locul guide to take you out. This is\nan adventure you don\'t want to miss\n5.6 mi\n1086\n3.9 mi.\n111 57.692\"\n36 50.604.\nWA\nLIFE\ntread lightly! UTAH\nELEVATED\n11154.111)\n36 56 242\nKANAB\nMOGICALLY USPOILE\nVETUL\nUT KANAB\nKACHLY USPORED','{\n  \"trail_name\": \"White Pocket\",\n  \"distance\": \"N/A\",\n  \"best_season\": \"Year Round\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"Easy Hike/Deep Sand\",\n  \"elevation\": \"5673 Feet\",\n  \"latitude\": \"36.93734\",\n  \"longitude\": \"-111.957692\",\n  \"accessibility\": \"High Clearance 4WD\",\n  \"pets_allowed\": \"Yes\",\n  \"reservation_required\": \"No\",\n  \"average_hours_to_hike\": \"Full Day\",\n  \"vehicle\": \"4WD / ATV\",\n  \"getting_there\": \"From Kanab, drive 38.6 miles east on HWY 89. Between milepost 26 and 25, you will see a dirt road that heads south. This is House Rock Valley Road, generally a very well-maintained hard packed dirt road. At 20.3 miles, turn left onto Road 1017 at the horse corral. From this point, high clearance, 4WD vehicles are required. Park and unload your ATV here. Let some air out of your tires to navigate the sandy terrain. Travel 6.2 miles on Road 1017. At the junction, continue straight on Road 1087. Drive 3.9 miles and stay left on Road 1036. Continue on Road 1086 for 5.6 miles to The White Pocket trailhead. Park at the trailhead and begin your short hike.\",\n  \"description\": \"Many visitors come to Southern Utah to do The Wave, one of the most photographed rock formations in the world! Unfortunately, due to its fragile nature, permits are limited to a maximum of 64 hikers a day. Many don\'t realize that The Wave is only the beginning of a long list of stunning formations the area has to offer. Locals prefer White Pocket, a much larger area than The Wave. A moonscape-like landscape that requires no permit. The swirling twisted, multi-colored natural artwork of White Pocket will fill your senses beyond imagination. Get an early start, as this is an all-day adventure. A 4WD, high clearance vehicle is a must for this journey, or hire a local guide to take you out. This is an adventure you don\'t want to miss!\",\n  \"description_html\": \"Many visitors come to Southern Utah to do The Wave, one of the most photographed rock formations in the world! Unfortunately, due to its fragile nature, permits are limited to a maximum of 64 hikers a day. Many don\'t realize that The Wave is only the beginning of a long list of stunning formations the area has to offer. Locals prefer White Pocket, a much larger area than The Wave. A moonscape-like landscape that requires no permit. The swirling twisted, multi-colored natural artwork of White Pocket will fill your senses beyond imagination. Get an early start, as this is an all-day adventure. A 4WD, high clearance vehicle is a must for this journey, or hire a local guide to take you out. This is an adventure you don\'t want to miss!\",\n  \"additional_info\": \"NOTE: Much of White Pocket is privately ranched. Please respect the privacy of others. Please do not disturb their property and animals.\\n\\nDISCLAIMER: This map and directions are i