fering everything from tents to luxury\naccommodations. Guided hikes, horseback,\nrappel, swim & other exciting activities.\nAMANGIRI\n1 KAYENTA RD,\nCANYON POINT, UT 84741\n435.675.3999\nAMAN.COM\nESCAPE TO A REMOTE, CONTEMPORARY\nHIDE-AWAY AMID AN ICONIC LANDSCAPE\nOF DRASTIC RED ROCK LAND SCULPTURE.\nAMAN OFFERS BOTH ADRENALINE-FUELED\nADVENTURE AND A PEACEFUL RESORT. \\n\\n KANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILED\nCheck locally for road\nand weather conditions.\nDo not drive on dirt\nroads when wet.\nUTAH ROAD\nCONDITIONS:\n800.492.2400\nST. GEORGE\nHurricane\nCedar\nCity\nKolob\nAIRPORTS\nLas Vegas\nSalt Lake City\nSt. George\nPhoenix\n(9)\nApple\nValley\nMILEAGE FROM KANAB\nAntelope Canyon\nBryce Canyon\nCoral Pink Sand Dunes\nDuck Creek Village\nGrand Canyon\nGrand Staircase\nHorseshoe Bend\nLake Powell\nMt.Carmel Junction\nPipe Spring\nZion\nSpringdale\n76 7 23 5578157 73 7 21 40\n77\n17\n▬▬▬▬▬▬▬▬‒‒‒‒‒‒▬▬▬▬▬▬▬▬▬▬▬▬▬▬\n200\n317\n80\n350\ntoo\nZION\nNATIONAL\nPARK\nHildale\n143\nCEDAR\nBREAKS\nNAT.\nMON.\nBrian Head\nCORAL PINK\nSAND DUNES\nSTATE PARK\nColorado City\nPipe Spring\nNational Monument *\nNavajo Lake\n389\nSALT LAKE CITY\nToroweap\n(143\nMount\nCarmel\nHatch\nGlendale\n89\n89\nDuck\nCreek\nVillage Alton\nΚΑΝΑΒ\nPanguitch\nPeekaboo\nSlot Canyon\nColorado River\nFredonia\nBest Friends\nMoqui Cave\n89\nDIXIE\nNATIONAL\nFOREST\nTropic\nReservoir BRYCE\non\nRed Canyon\nCANYON\nNATIONAL\nPARK\nSkutumpah Road,\nScenic Backway\nRuby\'s Inn\n4\nLick\nWash\nGRAND STAIRCASE\nUNIT\nInchworm\nArch\nKAIBAB\nNATIONAL\nFOREST\nLaFevre\nPoint\nTropic\nWillis\nGRAND CANYON\nNATIONAL\nPARK\nEagle\nmated\nJacob Lake\nNorth Rim\nOld★\nParia.\nDIXIE\nNATIONAL\nFOREST\nPine\nLake\nA\nHouse Rock Valley Road\nCannonville\nKodachrome Basin\n(12\nتحان\nPetrified\nForest\nState Park\n*Grosvenor Arch\nCottonwood\nNarrows KAIPAROWITS\nUNIT\nCottonwood Canyon\nScenic Backway\nEscalante\nBoulder STATE PARK\n12\nCalf Creek Falls\n1865\nLee\'s Ferry\nMarble Canyon\nVERMILION CLIFFS\nNATIONAL MONUMENT\nS HWY 89A mot\n86\nWahweep Hoodoos\nANASAZI\nINDIAN VILLAGE\nToadstools\nDAY\nMarina\nBig Water 89Wahweep\nWire Pass TH Coyote Buttes\nESCALANTE\nCANYON UNIT\nHole in the Rock\nScenic Backway\ny\nSmoky Mountain\nScenic Byway\n89\nDevils Garden\n***...*\nGlen Canyon Dam\nGLEN\nCANYON\nHole in the Rock\nScenic Backway NATIONAL\nSpooky Gulch RECREATION\nAREA\nChimney Rock\nPage\nHorseshoe Bend\n......\nAntelope Canyon\nCAPITOL\nREEF\nNATIONAL\nPARK\nDance Hall Rock\n$1.00\nTRETMA\nBurr\nTrail\n160\n98\nHole in the Rock\nLAKE POWELL\n*RAINBOW\nBRIDGE\nNATIONAL\nMONUMENT\nNAVAJO INDIAN\nRESERVATION\nBulffrog\nColorado River\nHalls\nCrossing\nMarina\n………………….………\nNavajo Indian\nReservation\nNavajo Indian\nReservation\nUTAH\nCLASSIFICATIONS\nARIZONA\nMonument\nValley\nInterstate Highway\nScenic Byways\nMajor Highways\nUnpaved Roads\n(276\nOld Monument Borders\nGrand Staircase Unit\nKaiparowits Unit\nEscalante Canyon Unit \\n\\n KANAB\nMAGICALLY UNSPOILED\nVISITSOUTHERNUTAH.COM\n78 South 100 East\nKanab, Utah 84741\nUTAH\nLIFE ELEVATEDⓇ\nUTAH OFFICE OF TOURISM',NULL,'2023-10-05 23:10:00','2023-10-05 23:10:00'),(74,'locations',121,'document','https://stateparks.utah.gov/stateparks/wp-content/uploads/sites/26/2015/02/CoralPink_Brochure.pdf','Address inquiries to:\nCoral Pink Sand Dunes State Park\nPO Box 95\nKanab, UT 84741-0095\n(435) 648-2800\n(800) 322-3770 Camping reservations\nUtah State Parks and Recreation\n1594 West North Temple, Ste. 116\nPO Box 146001\nSalt Lake City, UT 84114-6001\n(801) 538-7220\n(877) UT-PARKS (toll free)\n(801) 538-7458 (TTY)\nwww.stateparks.utah.gov\nInformation contained in this brochure was accurate at\ntime of printing. Trails, facilities, hours and regulations,\netc. change as mandated. For updated information,\nplease contact the park.\nUtah State Parks mission ...\nProvide opportunities to improve the quality of life\nin Utah through parks, programs and employees\nserving the public.\nThe Utah Department of Natural Resources receives\nfederal aid and prohibits discrimination on the basis of\nrace, color, sex, age, national origin or disability. For\ninformation or complaints regarding discrimination,\ncontact Executive Director, Utah Department of Natural\nResources, PO Box 145610, Salt Lake City, UT 84114-\n5610 or Equal Employment Opportunity Commission,\n1801 L Street, NW, Washington, DC 20507-0001.\n7/10 20M\nPrinted on recycled paper.\nDesign: Anne Wolfer Design Inc.\nPhoto Credits: Jerry Sintz\nUtah State Parks\nOFF-HIGHWAY VEHICLES\nUTAH\nDNR\nSTATE PARKS\nCoral Pink Sand Dunes offers 1,200 acres of open riding\narea for OHV enthusiasts. OHVs must be currently\nregistered and have an orange whiptail flag.\nAll operators must have a valid driver\'s license or\nstate-recognized certification, and riders 17 and under\nmust wear a properly sized and fastened helmet at all\ntimes. Utah State Park Rangers strongly encourage all\nriders to wear helmets. OHV riders should contact park\npersonnel for additional OHV laws and rules before\nventuring onto the dunes.\nReservations\nAdjacent to the park, riders will find hundreds of\nmiles of trails on lands managed by the USDI Bureau\nof Land Management.\nAdvance reservations are available for group-use and\nindividual campsites and are always recommended. Indi-\nvidual campsite reservations may be made a minimum of\ntwo days in advance of arrival date and up to 16 weeks\nin advance of park checkout date. Group reservations\nmay be made up to II months in advance.\nTo make a reservation, please call 322-3770 within\nSalt Lake City or toll-free (800) 322-3770. Unreserved\nsites are available on a first-come, first served basis.\nUser Fees\nDay-use and camping fees are charged, and additional\nfees are charged for reservations.\nGUIDELINES\nPlease observe these regulations to ensure that\neveryone enjoys their visit:\nRoads - Speed limits are 1) 45 mph on county roads,\n2) 15 mph on park roads and 3) 5 mph in the campground.\nThe campground loop is a one-way road to the right.\nVehicles must remain on pavement while in developed\nareas of the park.\nCamping - Camp only in designated campsites. Each permit\ncovers one vehicle and any attached recreational equipment.\nThere is an extra fee for additional vehicles or camping\nequipment. Up to eight people are allowed in a campsite.\nPlants and animals - All plants, animals, minerals, and other\nnatural features in state parks are protected. It is unlawful\nto remove, alter, destroy or molest them.\nVandalism - It is unlawful to mutilate or deface any natural\nfeature or structure. Please help keep your park beautiful.\nLittering - Please use refuse containers provided.\nFireworks - Explosives, fireworks or firecrackers are prohib-\nited unless authorized by the park manager.\nUtah State Parks\nCORAL PINK SAND\nDUNES STATE PARK\nFires - Campfires may be built in specified areas.\nMost developed campsites provide fire pits or grill\nfacilities. Collecting and gathering firewood within the\npark is prohibited. Firewood is available for purchase.\nPets - Pets are allowed in most Utah state parks,\nbut must be kept on a maximum six-foot leash.\nService animals are the only animals admitted in\npark buildings. For safety and courtesy, please\nkeep your pets under control.\nTTS RENT\nFirearms - Possession or use of firearms, traps or\nother devices capable of launching a projectile that\ncan immobilize, injure or kill a person or animal, or\ndamage property is prohibited unless the weapon or\ndevice is 1) unloaded and packed away to prevent its\nuse, 2) being used for the legal pursuit of wildlife or\n3) being used by authorized enforcement officers in\nthe performance of official duties.\nWastewater - It is unlawful to dump or to drain\nwater from campers or trailers onto the ground\nor into lakes and streams.\nQuiet hours - 10:00 p.m. to 9:00 a.m. (MST) \\n\\n CORAL PINK SANDU\nDUNES STATE PARK\nxplore acres of colorful, sweeping dunes dotted\nE fun af\nDunes State Park. Ride off-highway vehicles (OHVS)\nand dune buggies, or hike mountains of shifting sand.\nCoral Pink Sand Dunes State Park is a popular\ndestination for OHV enthusiasts who enjoy riding\n1,200 acres of dunes. Approximately 90 percent of\nthe Coral Pink dune field is open to OHV use.\nThe park was established in 1963 and serves as an\naccess point to the sand dunes for a variety of\nrecreation uses. Park rangers and staff work to\nprotect the resource and provide for public safety.\nGEOLOGY\nSand dunes are created by three factors: sand, high\nwinds, and a unique influence upon those high winds.\nAt Coral Pink Sand Dunes, a notch between the\nMoquith and Moccasin mountains causes this unique\ninfluence. Wind is funneled through this notch, increas-\ning wind velocity to a point where it can carry grains\nof sand from the eroding Navajo sandstone.\nThis phenomenon is known as the Venturi Effect.\nAfter the wind passes through the notch and into\nthe open valley, wind velocity decreases, depositing\nthe sand.\nPLANTS AND ANIMALS\nLocated at an elevation of 6,000 feet, Coral Pink Sand\nDunes receives relatively high amounts of precipita-\ntion. This moisture allows for a wide variety of plants,\nincluding stands of ponderosa pine, dune grasses more\nthan six feet tall, and beautiful wildflowers that reach\ntheir peak in June.\nCatch a glimpse of mule deer, coyotes, kit foxes jack-\nrabbits, and many small rodents within the park. The\ndunes support a diverse population of insects, including\nthe Coral Pink Tiger Beetle, which is found no where\nelse in the world. Melting snow often creates small\nponds on the dunes that support amphibians such as\nsalamanders and toads.\nNEARBY POINTS OF INTEREST\nCoral Pink Sand Dunes is an ideal location to set up a base camp\nfor exploring Utah\'s extraordinary scenery. Within a short drive\nare Cedar Breaks, Grand Staircase/Escalante, and Pipe Springs\nnational monuments, Kodachrome Basin State Park, Lake Powell,\nand Zion, Bryce Canyon and Grand Canyon national parks.\nHIKING\nWander the dunes and explore this interesting and unique land-\nscape. Discover many interesting hikes adjacent to the park, includ-\ning South Fork Indian Canyon Pictographs. These ancient Indian\nrock paintings are located at the bottom of South Fork Indian\nCanyon. The trailhead is approximately four miles northeast of the\npark and is accessible via foot, OHV and four-wheel drive vehicle.\nCONSERVATION AREA\nA 265-acre conservation parcel serves as a resource management\narea for protecting the Coral Pink Tiger Beetle and its habitat.\nThe remaining 1,200 acres dunes are open to all types of\nrecreational activities.\nCORAL PINK SAND DUNES STATE PARK\nN\nA\nOHV Day Use\nParking\nVisitor\nCenter\nHighway\n# #\nGroup\nSite\n#\nEmergency\nExit\n8\n5\n||\nCAMPING\nAdjacent to the dunes, camp in a modern campground\nwith restrooms, hot showers, and sewage disposal\nstation. Each roomy campsite provides pull-through\nparking, picnic table, and barbecue grill.\nBring a picnic lunch and select a day-use site, each with a\ntable and barbeque grill. A group day-use area adjacent to\nthe dunes offers a fire pit ideal for Dutch oven cooking.\nStroll along the one-half mile nature trail or the walkway,\nwhich extends onto the dunes and is accessible to\nvisitors of all abilities.\nA visitor center offers information on the park and\nsurrounding areas, OHV safety, and also offers an inter-\nesting display of sand specimens from around the world.\n10\nParking\nParking\nCampground\n13\n12\n2/3\n|\n40 #\n14\n19/20\nHost/\nSite\nParking\nMap Legend\n? Information\nPicnic Area\nRestrooms\nRV Dumping\nShowers\nViewpoint\nWater\nTrail\nOHV Dune\nAccess\n22\n21\n15/16\n17','Address:\r\nCoral Pink Sand Dunes State Park\r\nPO Box 95\r\nKanab, UT 84741-0095\r\nPhone: (435) 648-2800\r\nToll-free: (800) 322-3770 (Camping reservations)\r\n\r\nContact Information for Utah State Parks and Recreation:\r\n1594 West North Temple, Ste. 116\r\nPO Box 146001\r\nSalt Lake City, UT 84114-6001\r\nPhone: (801) 538-7220\r\nToll-free: (877) UT-PARKS\r\nTTY: (801) 538-7458\r\nWebsite: www.stateparks.utah.gov\r\n\r\nImportant Note:\r\nInformation in this brochure was accurate at the time of printing. Trails, facilities, hours, and regulations may change, so please contact the park for updated information.\r\n\r\nUtah State Parks Mission:\r\nTo provide opportunities to improve the quality of life in Utah through parks, programs, and employees serving the public.\r\n\r\nNon-discrimination Statement:\r\nThe Utah Department of Natural Resources receives federal aid and prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, sex, age, national origin, or disability. For information or complaints regarding discrimination, contact the Executive Director, Utah Department of Natural Resources, PO Box 145610, Salt Lake City, UT 84114-5610, or the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, 1801 L Street, NW, Washington, DC 20507-0001.\r\n\r\nGeneral Information:\r\n\r\nPrinted on recycled paper.\r\nDesign: Anne Wolfer Design Inc.\r\nPhoto Credits: Jerry Sintz\r\nOff-Highway Vehicles (OHV):\r\nCoral Pink Sand Dunes offers 1,200 acres of open riding area for OHV enthusiasts. OHVs must be currently registered and have an orange whiptail flag. All operators must have a valid driver\'s license or state-recognized certification, and riders 17 and under must wear a properly sized and fastened helmet at all times. Utah State Park Rangers strongly encourage all riders to wear helmets. OHV riders should contact park personnel for additional OHV laws and rules before venturing onto the dunes.\r\n\r\nReservations:\r\nAdjacent to the park, riders will find hundreds of miles of trails on lands managed by the USDI Bureau of Land Management. Advance reservations are available for group-use and individual campsites and are always recommended. Individual campsite reservations may be made a minimum of two days in advance of the arrival date and up to 16 weeks in advance of the park checkout date. Group reservations may be made up to 11 months in advance. To make a reservation, please call (800) 322-3770. Unreserved sites are available on a first-come, first-served basis.\r\n\r\nUser Fees:\r\nDay-use and camping fees are charged, and additional fees are charged for reservations.\r\n\r\nGuidelines:\r\nPlease observe these regulations to ensure that everyone enjoys their visit:\r\n\r\nRoads: Speed limits are 1) 45 mph on county roads, 2) 15 mph on park roads, and 3) 5 mph in the campground. The campground loop is a one-way road to the right. Vehicles must remain on pavement while in developed areas of the park.\r\nCamping: Camp only in designated campsites. Each permit covers one vehicle and any attached recreational equipment. There is an extra fee for additional vehicles or camping equipment. Up to eight people are allowed in a campsite.\r\nPlants and animals: All plants, animals, minerals, and other natural features in state parks are protected. It is unlawful to remove, alter, destroy, or molest them.\r\nVandalism: It is unlawful to mutilate or deface any natural feature or structure. Please help keep your park beautiful.\r\nLittering: Please use refuse containers provided.\r\nFireworks: Explosives, fireworks, or firecrackers are prohibited unless authorized by the park manager.\r\nFires: Campfires may be built in specified areas. Most developed campsites provide fire pits or grill facilities. Collecting and gathering firewood within the park is prohibited. Firewood is available for purchase.\r\nPets: Pets are allowed in most Utah state parks but must be kept on a maximum six-foot leash. Service animals are the only animals admitted in park buildings. For safety and courtesy, please keep your pets under control.\r\nFirearms: Possession or use of firearms, traps, or other devices capable of launching a projectile that can immobilize, injure, or kill a person or animal, or damage property is prohibited unless the weapon or device is 1) unloaded and packed away to prevent its use, 2) being used for the legal pursuit of wildlife, or 3) being used by authorized enforcement officers in the performance of official duties.\r\nWastewater: It is unlawful to dump or drain water from campers or trailers onto the ground or into lakes and streams.\r\nQuiet hours: 10:00 p.m. to 9:00 a.m. (MST)\r\nCoral Pink Sand Dunes State Park:\r\nExplore acres of colorful, sweeping dunes at Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park. Ride off-highway vehicles (OHVs) and dune buggies, or hike mountains of shifting sand. Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park is a popular destination for OHV enthusiasts who enjoy riding 1,200 acres of dunes. Approximately 90 percent of the Coral Pink dune field is open to OHV use. The park was established in 1963 and serves as an access point to the sand dunes for a variety of recreational uses. Park rangers and staff work to protect the resource and provide for public safety.\r\n\r\nGeology:\r\nSand dunes are created by three factors: sand, high winds, and a unique influence upon those high winds. At Coral Pink Sand Dunes, a notch between the Moquith and Moccasin mountains causes this unique influence. Wind is funneled through this notch, increasing wind velocity to a point where it can carry grains of sand from the eroding Navajo sandstone. This phenomenon is known as the Venturi Effect. After the wind passes through the notch and into the open valley, wind velocity decreases, depositing the sand.\r\n\r\nPlants and Animals:\r\nLocated at an elevation of 6,000 feet, Coral Pink Sand Dunes receives relatively high amounts of precipitation. This moisture allows for a wide variety of plants, including stands of ponderosa pine, dune grasses more than six feet tall, and beautiful wildflowers that reach their peak in June. Catch a glimpse of mule deer, coyotes, kit foxes, jackrabbits, and many small rodents within the park. The dunes support a diverse population of insects, including the Coral Pink Tiger Beetle, which is found nowhere else in the world. Melting snow often creates small ponds on the dunes that support amphibians such as salamanders and toads.\r\n\r\nNearby Points of Interest:\r\nCoral Pink Sand Dunes is an ideal location to set up a base camp for exploring Utah\'s extraordinary scenery. Within a short drive are Cedar Breaks, Grand Staircase/Escalante, and Pipe Springs national monuments, Kodachrome Basin State Park, Lake Powell, and Zion, Bryce Canyon, and Grand Canyon national parks.\r\n\r\nHiking:\r\nWander the dunes and explore this interesting and unique landscape. Discover many interesting hikes adjacent to the park, including South Fork Indian Canyon Pictographs. These ancient Indian rock paintings are located at the bottom of South Fork Indian Canyon. The trailhead is approximately four miles northeast of the park and is accessible via foot, OHV, and four-wheel-drive vehicles.\r\n\r\nConservation Area:\r\nA 265-acre conservation parcel serves as a resource management area for protecting the Coral Pink Tiger Beetle and its habitat. The remaining 1,200 acres of dunes are open to all types of recreational activities.','2023-10-05 23:13:09','2023-10-05 23:13:09'),(109,'trails',32,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Panorama Trail - Main Loop and enjoy magnificent views of Kodachrome Basin State Park, surrounding mountains, and even glimpses of amphitheaters in Bryce Canyon National Park while winding across the western side of the park and exploring interesting geological features such as the Ballerina Spire and the Hat Shop.','2023-10-09 22:59:34','2023-10-09 22:59:34'),(110,'trails',33,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Panorama Trail - Cool Cave Loop in Kodachrome Basin State Park and admire the impressive rock formations surrounding the basin as you gently ascend up and down sandy slopes, with the highlight being Cool Cave; this short loop offers panoramic views across the park.','2023-10-09 22:59:36','2023-10-09 22:59:36'),(111,'trails',34,'short',NULL,NULL,'Embark on the family-friendly Grand Parade Trail and marvel at its wide, flat path of firm sand as you take in the captivating sights of intriguing formations and awe-inspiring box canyons nestled beneath majestic red rock cliffs and towers.','2023-10-09 22:59:37','2023-10-09 22:59:37'),(112,'trails',35,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the family-friendly Alpine Pond Trail as you hike a scenic loop with gradual grades, enjoy the picturesque pond, and have the option to shorten the trail.','2023-10-09 22:59:38','2023-10-09 22:59:38'),(113,'trails',36,'short',NULL,NULL,'Embark on the Campground Trail, a short and easy hike that is perfect for the whole family, including your furry friend Fido, as it provides a quick link between the campground and visitor center while offering stunning views along the way.','2023-10-09 22:59:40','2023-10-09 22:59:40'),(114,'trails',6,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hiking Blowhard Mountain Trail #32047 is a 7-mile steep and rocky ascent that begins with a narrow canyon, offers stunning views along the edge of a cliff, passes through a sheep pasture, enters a pine forest, intersects with Potato Hollow Trail, wraps around a steep ridge, and finally leads to the Doppler radar station at the upper trailhead.','2023-10-09 23:00:10','2023-10-09 23:00:10'),(115,'trails',7,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Dark Hollow Trail #32032 to experience steep rugged descents, sharp rocky switchbacks, and blistering two track, while being rewarded with spectacular mountain vistas and beautiful red rock cliffs.','2023-10-09 23:00:11','2023-10-09 23:00:11'),(117,'trails',9,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Bristlecone Trail #32048, a very easy and family-friendly trail offering a short walk through a mesmerizing forest of bristlecone pines.','2023-10-09 23:00:14','2023-10-09 23:00:14'),(118,'trails',10,'short',NULL,NULL,'Cascade Falls Trail is a family-friendly hike offering stunning views of Zion National Park and leading to a most unique waterfall with short and little elevation gain.','2023-10-09 23:00:15','2023-10-09 23:00:15'),(123,'trails',15,'short',NULL,NULL,'Embark on the Bristlecone Loop, a family-friendly trail showcasing stunning scenery, diverse plant life, abundant wildlife, and breathtaking views of the Four Corners area, while marveling at the unique presence of rare Bristlecone Pines.','2023-10-09 23:00:34','2023-10-09 23:00:34'),(129,'trails',21,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Riggs Spring Loop Trail, a two-day outing that offers peaceful views of pink cliffs, eroded hoodoos, and dense stands of ponderosa pines. Start at Rainbow Point parking area and head west towards Yovimpa Pass, descending through forested terrain with great views. Camp at the shady Riggs Spring, wake early to catch the sunrise on the rim, then turn north to ascend the Mutton Valley drainage and explore the base of more pink cliffs. Enjoy solitude and wildlife sightings before reaching the Corral Hollow campsite. Continue the traverse, reaching the breezy saddle below the Promontory mesa. Finally, begin the climb back out through drier forests, passing junctions with the Under-the-Rim Trail and the Bristlecone Loop until reaching the parking area. This 2-day backpacking trip showcases uniquely forested terrain and is filled with scenic beauty.','2023-10-09 23:01:52','2023-10-09 23:01:52'),(130,'trails',22,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Rim Trail, a family-friendly trail with easy grades, info markers, and lookout platforms, offering movie-like scenery that will leave kids awe-inspired and parents in awe.','2023-10-09 23:01:53','2023-10-09 23:01:53'),(131,'trails',23,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Sheep Creek Connecting Trail from the Swamp Canyon overlook pullout, passing through dense forest and circumnavigating a butte before descending into Sheep Creek drainage, where you\'ll see hoodoos and traverse a wooded canyon, eventually meeting the Under-the-Rim Trail and the Sheep Creek Trail at a 4-way intersection at the canyon bottom.','2023-10-09 23:01:55','2023-10-09 23:01:55'),(133,'trails',25,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Swamp Canyon Connecting Trail from the Swamp Canyon overlook pullout, heading south and descending below the canyon rim. Traverse through a forested valley between towering stone formations until reaching the junction with the Under-the-Rim Trail near the Right Fork Swamp Canyon campsite, where you\'ll be able to see Swamp Canyon Butte to the south. This trail is ideal for accessing the Under-the-Rim Trail and Right Fork Camp for a loop hike.','2023-10-09 23:02:02','2023-10-09 23:02:02'),(134,'trails',26,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Bryce Canyon to Red Canyon Bike Trail, a popular and well-maintained paved path that winds through Bryce Canyon National Park and follows the highway to Red Canyon, offering a mellow and scenic experience with no obstacles and beautiful surroundings from Inspiration Point to the bottom of Red Canyon.','2023-10-09 23:02:03','2023-10-09 23:02:03'),(136,'trails',28,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Agua Canyon Connecting Trail and enjoy broad views of the rim, navigate switchbacks to pass pink limestone cliffs, and arrive at a junction with the Under-the-Rim Trail, where you can enjoy views of multicolored Agua Canyon and Natural Bridge, with Rainbow Point above the skyline to the south. Connects the Under-the-Rim Trail with the main park road at Ponderosa Point.','2023-10-09 23:02:07','2023-10-09 23:02:07'),(138,'trails',30,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike up the beautiful rugged canyon to a large sandstone peak and then continue out along a mesa to witness the amazing Eye of Heaven Arch at Squirrel Creek to Eye of Heaven Arch Park.','2023-10-09 23:02:35','2023-10-09 23:02:35'),(139,'trails',37,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Rattlesnake Creek Trail #32051 to see the Cedar Breaks boundary fence, alpine meadows, spruce and fir forests, beautiful views of Cedar Breaks and Ashdown Gorge formations, mixed conifer and grass and aspen drainages, a west-facing bare slope, mature spruce and fir stands, Stud Flat with panoramic views, a rock conifer stand, Rattlesnake Creek with ponderosa pine, pinyon, juniper, and fir trees, High Mountain Trail #052, Ashdown Gorge\'s rock channel, private land, Ashdown road, and Potato Hollow Trail #050 for a descent of 2,500 feet in four miles to Ashdown Creek.','2023-10-09 23:02:39','2023-10-09 23:02:39'),(141,'trails',39,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the switchbacking uphill portion of the Lava Flow loop on the Lichen It trail for stunning views of the surrounding landscape.','2023-10-09 23:02:42','2023-10-09 23:02:42'),(142,'trails',40,'short',NULL,NULL,'\"Experience the Ghost Flats #7 trail and witness mesmerizing red rock formations, striking desert landscapes, and breathtaking views of the southern Utah and northern Arizona region.\"','2023-10-09 23:02:43','2023-10-09 23:02:43'),(143,'trails',41,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Lightning Switch trail, being aware of and courteous to other users, and experience a tight switchback climb that offers a great view of Cedar City.','2023-10-09 23:02:44','2023-10-09 23:02:44'),(144,'trails',42,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Boulder Dash trail to see breathtaking views of towering cliffs and dramatic rock formations.','2023-10-09 23:02:45','2023-10-09 23:02:45'),(145,'trails',43,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the breathtaking views of Thunder Mountain on the mostly smooth hard pack trail with short intense sections of switchbacks, and enjoy the many swooping hills and valleys along the way for incredible photo opportunities.','2023-10-09 23:02:46','2023-10-09 23:02:46'),(146,'trails',44,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Golden Wall Trail #33095, an easily accessible and family-friendly trail near the visitor center, offering a scenic journey through ponderosa pine forests and stunning golden and red rock walls.','2023-10-09 23:02:49','2023-10-09 23:02:49'),(147,'trails',45,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Castle Bridge Trail #33096, offering a close-up view of fantastic formations as an awe-inspiring alternative to one section of the Golden Wall Trail.','2023-10-09 23:02:50','2023-10-09 23:02:50'),(149,'trails',47,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Pink Ledges Trail, a short, self-guided interpretive trail near the road, showcasing unusual red rock formations such as hoodoos and alcoves near the Visitor Center.','2023-10-09 23:02:53','2023-10-09 23:02:53'),(150,'trails',48,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Birdseye Trail #33097, a family-friendly hike showcasing stunning and unique rock formations close to the highway, offering up-close glimpses of the region\'s finest red-rock formations near the Visitor Center.','2023-10-09 23:02:55','2023-10-09 23:02:55'),(151,'trails',49,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the short Photo Trail for family-friendly views of nearby formations and the surrounding area from a ridge.','2023-10-09 23:02:56','2023-10-09 23:02:56'),(152,'trails',50,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hoodoo Trail Family Friendly - Explore a short and easy trail, passing towering red hoodoos near the Visitor Center.','2023-10-09 23:02:57','2023-10-09 23:02:57'),(153,'trails',51,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the wonder of an array of rock formations along the Arches Trail #33075, where small children can explore and discover beautiful hoodoos, arches, and turrets that will ignite their imagination.','2023-10-09 23:02:58','2023-10-09 23:02:58'),(154,'trails',52,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike along Cassidy Trail #33093 to see stunning rock formations, sweeping views of the desert landscape, and the scenic beauty of southern Utah and northern Arizona.','2023-10-09 23:02:59','2023-10-09 23:02:59'),(155,'trails',53,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore Rich Trail #33092 and marvel at its stunning landscapes, including rugged canyons, towering rock formations, and panoramic views of the surrounding desert.','2023-10-09 23:03:01','2023-10-09 23:03:01'),(156,'trails',54,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the easy multiuse Casto Canyon Trail #33089, winding along a sandy wash through stunning red rock formations, offering scenic views and connecting to the Fremont Trail and Cassidy Trail.','2023-10-09 23:03:03','2023-10-09 23:03:03'),(158,'trails',59,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore Powell Point Trail as it meanders south through ancient bristlecone pine atop a massive limestone cliff, offering great views along the way, and be sure to take it to the end for the incredible Powell Point viewpoint.','2023-10-09 23:03:07','2023-10-09 23:03:07'),(159,'trails',60,'short',NULL,NULL,'Enjoy a scenic hike along the Grand View Trail #601, meandering through aspen stands, black timber, and meadows, passing by a pond, an old sheep camp with three metal beds, a spring/bog, and a creek, crossing over the Proctor Canyon FS road, and continuing through the steepest section between Proctor and Johnson Canyon, with a caution to follow the trail west at the old pole fence and trough junction, and crossing FS642 at Hillsdale Canyon before reaching Thunder Mountain Trail #33098 for a gradual downhill through the Ponderosas, offering breathtaking views of the eastern pink cliffs and the western valley floor.','2023-10-09 23:03:11','2023-10-09 23:03:11'),(160,'trails',61,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike from Pine Valley campgrounds to a viewpoint overlooking Pine Valley, passing through pine forests and climbing for about 1 1/2 miles before turning east for the remaining distance.','2023-10-09 23:03:12','2023-10-09 23:03:12'),(162,'trails',63,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Big Hole Loop, a unique trail showcasing the old iron ore mines.','2023-10-09 23:03:14','2023-10-09 23:03:14'),(163,'trails',64,'short',NULL,NULL,'Take a shortcut along the Race Course Loop to enjoy the Sunnyside singletrack trail and experience scenic views.','2023-10-09 23:03:15','2023-10-09 23:03:15'),(165,'trails',66,'short',NULL,NULL,'Lost World Trail at Three Peaks Recreation Area showcases stunning rock features for visitors to explore.','2023-10-09 23:03:17','2023-10-09 23:03:17'),(166,'trails',67,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Outer Limits Trail, a narrow and rolling path perfect for beginners, offering stunning views of the surrounding landscapes.','2023-10-09 23:03:18','2023-10-09 23:03:18'),(168,'trails',69,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Twilight Zone Trail, a dynamic and diverse route showcasing a myriad of sights and experiences.','2023-10-09 23:03:20','2023-10-09 23:03:20'),(170,'trails',71,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Moby Dick Trail to witness stunning views of towering red cliffs and sweeping desert landscapes.','2023-10-09 23:03:22','2023-10-09 23:03:22'),(171,'trails',75,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Mamie Creek Natural Bridge Trail, following Mamie Creek for about 0.8 miles until reaching the bridge, which is impossible to miss as the creek runs through it, offering a short side trip to a stunning natural bridge.','2023-10-09 23:03:23','2023-10-09 23:03:23'),(172,'trails',77,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Petrified Forest Trail and witness the breathtaking sight of incredible pieces of petrified wood.','2023-10-09 23:03:24','2023-10-09 23:03:24'),(173,'trails',78,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Trail of Sleeping Rainbows for a more challenging extension to the main loop, where breathtaking panoramic views of Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument accompany an abundance of remarkable petrified wood in various sizes and shapes.','2023-10-09 23:03:26','2023-10-09 23:03:26'),(174,'trails',80,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the stunning beauty of towering sandstone walls, massive cottonwood trees, and breathtaking geology as you journey through the extremely hot and occasionally dry riverbed from the confluence of Mamie Creek and the Escalante River to the old Mail Trail.','2023-10-09 23:03:27','2023-10-09 23:03:27'),(175,'trails',83,'short',NULL,NULL,'Descend steeply on White Creek Trail #40, following the creek to Beck Hollow, where picturesque views await.','2023-10-09 23:03:28','2023-10-09 23:03:28'),(177,'trails',85,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the family-friendly Hurricane Wash trail, journeying through the towering walls of the canyon to discover shade and water at the end.','2023-10-09 23:03:30','2023-10-09 23:03:30'),(178,'trails',88,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the family-friendly Wahweep Hoodoos trail, where you can admire fantastic formations that inspire magical descriptions, as witnessed by a local store owner and her three-year-old daughter, who hiked to a fairyland of toadstools, hoodoos, white ghosts, and floatstone-type formations.','2023-10-09 23:03:32','2023-10-09 23:03:32'),(181,'trails',97,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the most technical trail in the Gooseberry Mesa trail network as you hike the South Rim Trail, which undulates up and down house size sandstone mounds, twists through tight rock canyons, and dances on the edge in several places, offering breathtaking vistas along the way.','2023-10-09 23:03:38','2023-10-09 23:03:38'),(182,'trails',98,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the North Rim Trail, a technical and challenging hike that winds through tight rock canyons, undulating up and down house-size sandstone mounds, offering breathtaking vistas and the option to connect with other trails for a longer or shorter hike.','2023-10-09 23:03:40','2023-10-09 23:03:40'),(183,'trails',99,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the White Trail, a mostly flat doubletrack that passes through the heart of Gooseberry Mesa, offering access to various trails in the Gooseberry network, while showcasing a diverse surface of hard packed dirt, sand, and slickrock.','2023-10-09 23:03:41','2023-10-09 23:03:41'),(184,'trails',100,'short',NULL,NULL,'\"Explore Bowls and Ledges, a nature\'s roller coaster connecting the North Rim Train to the Practice Trail, with branching options onto the Windmill Trail, offering a fun warm-up and a chance to see many bowls and ledges along the way.\"','2023-10-09 23:03:43','2023-10-09 23:03:43'),(185,'trails',101,'short',NULL,NULL,'Start your Gooseberry Mesa adventure with the Practice Trail, a challenging but enjoyable warm-up before tackling the main trails, offering undulating sandstone mounds and twisting rock canyons for a technical hike at 5200 feet elevation, marked by white paint dots and signs at the junctions.','2023-10-09 23:03:44','2023-10-09 23:03:44'),(186,'trails',102,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Windmill Trail, a traditional singletrack that offers a pleasant respite from the slickrock terrain, showcasing beautiful views after a day of exploring the other Gooseberry trails.','2023-10-09 23:03:46','2023-10-09 23:03:46'),(187,'trails',103,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the thrilling speed and smooth flow of the JEM Trail, while enjoying scenic views of the surrounding landscapes.','2023-10-09 23:03:47','2023-10-09 23:03:47'),(188,'trails',104,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Dead Ringer trail in the Hurricane Cliffs Trail network, completed in 2015 through a partnership between the Dixie Mountain Bike Trails Association and the BLM, offering an easy climb to the JEM parking lot and a smooth and easy descent with great views, along a narrow bench with limited passing space, allowing hikers to combine it with JEM Trail or Goulds Rim trail for loops over 20 miles.','2023-10-09 23:03:50','2023-10-09 23:03:50'),(189,'trails',107,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Cryptobionic Family Friendly trail, a fun and smooth graded pathway that offers an easy hike, perfect for combining with other trails, with stunning views and enjoyable surroundings.','2023-10-09 23:03:51','2023-10-09 23:03:51'),(190,'trails',108,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Secret Trail and marvel at the scenic view of the mesa\'s edge while traversing a delightful singletrack of slick rock and dirt.','2023-10-09 23:03:52','2023-10-09 23:03:52'),(191,'trails',109,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the North Point Loop on the Magic Carpet trail to experience a solid lollipop hike with nice singletrack, some rougher sections, and stunning views of Gooseberry Mesa along the way, while being mindful of other trail users including mountain bikers.','2023-10-09 23:03:53','2023-10-09 23:03:53'),(192,'trails',110,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the exhilarating Goosebump trail as it winds along the mesmerizing contours of the mesa, offering breathtaking views, an exciting roller coaster-like route with frequent short climbs and descents, occasional rugged sections (Blue), and fun smooth terrains (Green), before concluding at a junction with jeep and ATV trails.','2023-10-09 23:03:55','2023-10-09 23:03:55'),(193,'trails',111,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the More Cowbell trail for a fantastic beginner loop connecting to the JEM Trail and Dead Ringer trail, offering users a fun and successful experience with smooth and wide paths and breathtaking vistas in all directions.','2023-10-09 23:03:56','2023-10-09 23:03:56'),(194,'trails',112,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Goosebump Connector, a straightforward trail connecting the Goosebump and Jems trails, providing hikers with scenic views of the surrounding Utah and Arizona landscapes.','2023-10-09 23:03:58','2023-10-09 23:03:58'),(195,'trails',113,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the stunning outside loop of Holy Guacamole, where hikers will see the iridescent green mesa, glowing sage brush, wildflowers in certain seasons, juniper, mesquite, sagebrush, rabbit brush, and breathtaking views of Eagle Crags and the valley.','2023-10-09 23:03:59','2023-10-09 23:03:59'),(196,'trails',114,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike Lime Trail to witness breathtaking views of West Temple and Mount Kinesava, with the possibility of spotting a California Condor soaring overhead in this fun and primarily slick rock trail.','2023-10-09 23:04:01','2023-10-09 23:04:01'),(197,'trails',115,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Three Falls Trail, scramble through a river gorge, and discover awesome views of tall canyon walls and three dry, slick rock waterfalls.','2023-10-09 23:04:02','2023-10-09 23:04:02'),(198,'trails',116,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Wire Mesa Loop to experience a diverse terrain with a greater variety than other mesa trails, offering stunning views of southern Utah and northern Arizona.','2023-10-09 23:04:03','2023-10-09 23:04:03'),(199,'trails',117,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Margarita Trail, beginning above Dalton Wash on a mesa and navigating slickrock marked by frequent cairns and rock-filled baskets, with glimpses into the lush Dalton Wash along the way, leading to breathtaking views of Cougar Mountain and neighboring peaks, before merging with the Guacamole Trail for a thrilling adventure over slickrock and stunning vistas.','2023-10-09 23:04:05','2023-10-09 23:04:05'),(200,'trails',120,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the renovated Tom\'s Canyon Trail, an intermediate singletrack trail with minimal technical features and quick ups and downs, offering stunning views of the canyon and La Estancia housing development from the viewpoint labeled \"Bench,\" located along the trail.','2023-10-09 23:04:07','2023-10-09 23:04:07'),(201,'trails',121,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike along Cottonwood Canyon Trail #22 and marvel at the winding path through juniper trees, nestled at the base of stunning red cliffs.','2023-10-09 23:04:08','2023-10-09 23:04:08'),(202,'trails',122,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Sherry Belle Trail starting from the Sherryville trailhead off of Terrel Avenue, descending south towards the reservoir where you will cross a road to join the main loop around the water, experiencing a pleasant, mostly flat trail that leads to a park with crossfit equipment, a beach volleyball court, and artificial bouldering rocks, before crossing the dam and reconnecting back to the trail that follows the paved path circling Kanab and Jackson Flat Reservoir.','2023-10-09 23:04:11','2023-10-09 23:04:11'),(203,'trails',123,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the K-Hill Trail which begins on the east side, accessible from a quaint parking area at the end of a paved residential road, and takes you on an exhilarating journey along the side of the breathtaking cliffs of Kanab, offering magnificent views throughout, as you traverse up and down periodically along a shelf at the base of the cliffs, before eventually connecting with Tom\'s Canyon Trail and concluding in another residential area; pair this trail with other neighboring trails for a more extended and memorable hiking experience.','2023-10-09 23:04:13','2023-10-09 23:04:13'),(204,'trails',124,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Pugh Canyon trail, starting at the end of a residential road, heading east through a narrowing canyon filled with vibrant red cliffs and a creekbed, witnessing an abundance of vegetation as you progress, and ultimately reaching a stunning 35-foot waterfall where a gentle flow creates a captivating sight.','2023-10-09 23:04:15','2023-10-09 23:04:15'),(205,'trails',125,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Squaw Trail starting at Jacob Hamblin Park, ascending up a canyon and following a narrow wash to see amazing cliffs above Kanab, while enjoying a rocky and somewhat technical ascent with some scrambling required.','2023-10-09 23:04:17','2023-10-09 23:04:17'),(206,'trails',126,'short',NULL,NULL,'Kanarra Creek Trail offers a family-friendly 4.2-mile hike where hikers can relax in a refreshing pool and enjoy a beautiful waterfall at the end.','2023-10-09 23:04:18','2023-10-09 23:04:18'),(207,'trails',127,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Spring Creek Route, a scenic trail featuring a spring and a flowing wash in the initial mile.','2023-10-09 23:04:19','2023-10-09 23:04:19'),(208,'trails',132,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Cobra Arch Trail, managed by BLM Utah - Kanab Field Office, which follows the stunning Paria Canyon rim before descending to witness the mesmerizing beauty of a unique sandstone arch.','2023-10-09 23:04:20','2023-10-09 23:04:20'),(209,'trails',133,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the awe-inspiring beauty of The Wave as you hike through the otherworldly sandstone formation in southern Utah and witness its mesmerizing colors and unique geological features.','2023-10-09 23:04:21','2023-10-09 23:04:21'),(210,'trails',134,'short',NULL,NULL,'Embark on a breathtaking journey through a stunning slot canyon that narrows at points, ultimately leading to the magnificent Buckskin Gulch.','2023-10-09 23:04:22','2023-10-09 23:04:22'),(211,'trails',135,'short',NULL,NULL,'Embark on the wildest hike you\'ll ever experience as you navigate through Buckskin Gulch, a 14-mile non-technical slot canyon nestled deep in the Utah Desert, where GPS becomes ineffective and the sky remains barely visible, creating an extraordinary and challenging trek.','2023-10-09 23:04:24','2023-10-09 23:04:24'),(212,'trails',136,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike and wade along an alkaline river with quicksand, and encounter various bird species and lizards on the White House Trail, covering a distance of eight to ten miles.','2023-10-09 23:04:26','2023-10-09 23:04:26'),(213,'trails',137,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Whiterocks trail for a refreshing change from the iconic red rock landscapes, and witness stunning natural formations and diverse flora along the way.','2023-10-09 23:04:27','2023-10-09 23:04:27'),(214,'trails',138,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Lava Flow Trail and discover a surprisingly vegetated lava field.','2023-10-09 23:04:27','2023-10-09 23:04:27'),(215,'trails',139,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Butterfly Trail, a scenic path that winds around a sandstone butte, offering breathtaking views of the surrounding desert landscape.','2023-10-09 23:04:28','2023-10-09 23:04:28'),(216,'trails',140,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience Whiterocks Amphitheater\'s great sandstone pothole on a brief hike.','2023-10-09 23:04:29','2023-10-09 23:04:29'),(217,'trails',141,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the challenging Padre Canyon Trail, following several cairns in the wash to reach a saddle, where breathtaking views of the White Cliffs section of the park await.','2023-10-09 23:04:31','2023-10-09 23:04:31'),(218,'trails',142,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Red Sands Trail, which requires ample time and water to navigate the deep sand in the dry wash and explore the diverse desert landscape.','2023-10-09 23:04:32','2023-10-09 23:04:32'),(219,'trails',143,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the wonders of Snow Canyon Drive on the Whiptail Trail, a scenic paved path perfect for cyclists and pedestrians, providing a great option for returning to your car after exploring the Padre Canyon Trail.','2023-10-09 23:04:33','2023-10-09 23:04:33'),(220,'trails',144,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the amazing features on the petrified dunes of the Petrified Dunes Trail, which photographers love, and enjoy great views from the high point, while practicing Leave No Trace and only observing the Moqui Balls, without taking any as it is not permitted.','2023-10-09 23:04:35','2023-10-09 23:04:35'),(221,'trails',145,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the dirt road of West Canyon Road, managed by Utah State Parks - Snow Canyon, offering hikers scenic views while being closed to motorized traffic.','2023-10-09 23:04:36','2023-10-09 23:04:36'),(222,'trails',146,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore Hidden Pinyon Overlook Spur in Snow Canyon State Park and enjoy breathtaking views of the surrounding landscapes.','2023-10-09 23:04:37','2023-10-09 23:04:37'),(223,'trails',147,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Johnson Arch Trail, a short and easy trail leading to a rather large natural arch, offering stunning views.','2023-10-09 23:04:37','2023-10-09 23:04:37'),(224,'trails',148,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Scout Cave Trail, which can be followed in either direction, connecting Snow Canyon to the Paradise Canyon Area, revealing varied terrain and a shallow cave in the middle.','2023-10-09 23:04:39','2023-10-09 23:04:39'),(225,'trails',149,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the beautiful Quail Creek trail, which connects with Anasazi and the north end of Prospector, offering stunning views of the surrounding area.','2023-10-09 23:04:40','2023-10-09 23:04:40'),(226,'trails',150,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the super technical Gila Trail connecting to the Paradise Rim trail system, revealing stunning views of the surrounding landscape.','2023-10-09 23:04:41','2023-10-09 23:04:41'),(227,'trails',151,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike Turtle Wall to see stunning red rock formations and enjoy panoramic views of the surrounding desert landscape.','2023-10-09 23:04:42','2023-10-09 23:04:42'),(228,'trails',152,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Paradise Rim Trail to experience breathtaking panoramic views of the surrounding landscape.','2023-10-09 23:04:43','2023-10-09 23:04:43'),(229,'trails',153,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Chuckwalla trail and soak in the breathtaking views of red rock formations, desert flora, and possibly spot some wildlife along the way.','2023-10-09 23:04:44','2023-10-09 23:04:44'),(230,'trails',154,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Anasazi Trail, pay the $5 day use fee, and discover the interpreted heritage site, Red Cliffs Archaeological Site.','2023-10-09 23:04:45','2023-10-09 23:04:45'),(231,'trails',155,'short',NULL,NULL,'Red Reef Trail day use fee includes a popular trail leading to a nearby swimming hole or a backcountry trek that connects Quail Creek with Cottonwood Canyon, offering hikers stunning views of the scenic landscapes.','2023-10-09 23:04:46','2023-10-09 23:04:46'),(232,'trails',156,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Cinder Cone Trail inside Snow Canyon State Park boundary, where you will pass some lava flows and ascend to the rim of an old volcano cinder cone, offering panoramic views.','2023-10-09 23:04:47','2023-10-09 23:04:47'),(233,'trails',157,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the tricky Cottonwood Hills Trail for a chance to see soft silt and small rocks.','2023-10-09 23:04:48','2023-10-09 23:04:48'),(234,'trails',158,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Beck Hill Trail, an open and sandy ascent connecting the Chuckwalla, Paradise Rim Trail, and Gila Trail, revealing breathtaking views along the way.','2023-10-09 23:04:49','2023-10-09 23:04:49'),(235,'trails',159,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike along the High Grade Ridge top trail to connect the main parking area near I-15 with the Prospector Trail, offering stunning views of the surrounding area.','2023-10-09 23:04:50','2023-10-09 23:04:50'),(236,'trails',160,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the twisty and rolling 7.1 mile long Prospector Trail to experience scenic views of the surrounding landscape.','2023-10-09 23:04:51','2023-10-09 23:04:51'),(237,'trails',161,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Broken Mesa Rim Trail, a rocky 14.5-mile loop climbing a total of 1300ft, located 5 miles north of St. George, offering stunning views along the way.','2023-10-09 23:04:54','2023-10-09 23:04:54'),(238,'trails',162,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Icehouse Trail for a six-mile downhill adventure from the base of the Pine Valley Mountains to Washington, with stunning views along the way.','2023-10-09 23:04:55','2023-10-09 23:04:55'),(239,'trails',163,'short',NULL,NULL,'Embark on the Dino Cliffs Trail to witness breathtaking vistas and ancient dinosaur tracks along a good short slickrock section.','2023-10-09 23:04:56','2023-10-09 23:04:56'),(240,'trails',164,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Grapevine Trail and see Church Rocks, while traversing a short doubletrack connector.','2023-10-09 23:04:57','2023-10-09 23:04:57'),(241,'trails',165,'short',NULL,NULL,'Embark on the Owen Loop for a thrilling hike, where adventurous explorers will witness breathtaking views of the city while traversing winding red rock trails.','2023-10-09 23:04:58','2023-10-09 23:04:58'),(242,'trails',166,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the scenic City Creek Trail, where sand and red rock lead to breathtaking views of the town and the majestic hills and rock formations that surround it.','2023-10-09 23:04:59','2023-10-09 23:04:59'),(243,'trails',167,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hiking the family-friendly Brooks Nature Trail reveals a short yet diverse trail, showcasing the stunning riparian area leading to Owen\'s Loop.','2023-10-09 23:05:00','2023-10-09 23:05:00'),(244,'trails',168,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore Mustang Pass, a rocky singletrack trail that winds through charred post-fire desert, offering breathtaking views of the surrounding rocks.','2023-10-09 23:05:01','2023-10-09 23:05:01'),(245,'trails',169,'short',NULL,NULL,'Take a short and enjoyable hike through Church Rocks, where you will encounter mostly smooth rocks along the trail.','2023-10-09 23:05:02','2023-10-09 23:05:02'),(246,'trails',170,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Bracken Connector trail to witness stunning desert landscapes and experience breathtaking views of southern Utah and northern Arizona.','2023-10-09 23:05:03','2023-10-09 23:05:03'),(247,'trails',171,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience an unrunnable slog through deep sand along Spanish Wash, witnessing stunning desert landscapes.','2023-10-09 23:05:04','2023-10-09 23:05:04'),(248,'trails',172,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Historic Babylon Trail, where you\'ll find an obvious gap in the cliffs to park and interesting petroglyphs to the left just before exiting the gap, then follow the trail through heavy vegetation, uphill across varied terrain, watching for small cairns, until reaching a barbed wire fence that leads to the open desert trail of the Little Purgatory Trail, offering no shade but starting with intriguing petroglyphs.','2023-10-09 23:05:06','2023-10-09 23:05:06'),(249,'trails',173,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Little Purgatory Trail early in the morning for a full exposure to the sun and enjoy great views of Sandstone Mountain and the Red Cliffs along the open desert trail including a very long and steep section.','2023-10-09 23:05:08','2023-10-09 23:05:08'),(250,'trails',174,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore The Vortex trail to witness stunning views of Dammeron Valley and Pine Mountain amidst the red rock country, while being cautious of the muddy spots on the gravel road after rainfall.','2023-10-09 23:05:09','2023-10-09 23:05:09'),(251,'trails',175,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Chinle Trail, starting from the Anasazi Way sub-division, as it winds through the open desert, offering incredible views of cliffs like the West Temple and Mount Kinesava, and crossing areas of petrified wood and highly developed cryptobiotic soil, leading hikers to the narrows, pools, and waterfalls nestled between enormous boulders and ultimately to the base of Mount Kinesava.','2023-10-09 23:05:12','2023-10-09 23:05:12'),(252,'trails',176,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Eagle Crags Trail for short, breathtaking views of Zion and the surrounding canyons, while enjoying the rough, but accessible, BLM-managed access road.','2023-10-09 23:05:13','2023-10-09 23:05:13'),(253,'trails',177,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the rolling Barrel Roll trail while enjoying excellent views of Santa Clara and St. George, as well as the Red Mountain, Pine Valley Mountains, Santa Clara River corridor, and the desert mesas of the Santa Clara River Reserve.','2023-10-09 23:05:15','2023-10-09 23:05:15'),(254,'trails',178,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Sidewinder trail to fully experience awe-inspiring vistas along a narrow mesa, while winding through switchbacks.','2023-10-09 23:05:16','2023-10-09 23:05:16'),(255,'trails',179,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the breathtaking views and unique terrain of Precipice Trail, as it winds through stunning desert landscapes, presenting awe-inspiring vantages and moments of exposure, all while navigating the tight squeeze created by massive rock slabs tumbling down from the upper rim.','2023-10-09 23:05:18','2023-10-09 23:05:18'),(256,'trails',180,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Suicidal Tendencies trail, navigating steep switchbacks and exposed sections while descending from Sidewinder Mesa, then ascend to encounter Hangover and Thread the Needle before reaching the top of the Mesa, where gentle switchbacks offer views of the Beaver Dam Mountains, followed by a descent through Juniper trees to complete the loop, being mindful of other users and desert wildlife.','2023-10-09 23:05:20','2023-10-09 23:05:20'),(257,'trails',181,'short',NULL,NULL,'\"Embark on the Pushing Tin Loop, an enjoyable and straightforward multi-use singletrack trail showcasing beautiful morning views, occasional airplanes overhead, and notable landmarks such as Will Gilbert Point, where hikers can rest on a folding bench-table facing southwest, at around two miles into the trail.\"','2023-10-09 23:05:22','2023-10-09 23:05:22'),(258,'trails',182,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the short and technical loop of the Kentucky Lucky Chicken Trail, where stunning views of the surrounding landscapes await.','2023-10-09 23:05:23','2023-10-09 23:05:23'),(259,'trails',183,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the fun lollipop loop of Rim Rock Trail out of the Sand Cove Trailhead and marvel at the stunning rock formations.','2023-10-09 23:05:24','2023-10-09 23:05:24'),(260,'trails',184,'short',NULL,NULL,'Add a sweet side loop to Rim Reaper, offering more singletrack and showcasing beautiful views of the surrounding rim.','2023-10-09 23:05:25','2023-10-09 23:05:25'),(261,'trails',185,'short',NULL,NULL,'During the Rim Runner Trail, hikers will encounter a split from Rim Ramble 1.6 miles into the hike, and by combining this loop trail with Rim Rock Trail, they will experience more sweet singletrack and enjoy stunning vistas on firm coarse sandy soil with large rounded rocks, as well as a delightful side loop with grand vistas in every direction.','2023-10-09 23:05:27','2023-10-09 23:05:27'),(262,'trails',186,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the 2.5-mile-long Temple Quarry Trail, located in the heart of town, offering spectacular birds-eye views of St. George and significant historical importance as it was used by Mormon Pioneers to access the quarry for constructing the foundation of the St. George Temple, with benches, historical markers, and a loop showcasing the wagons used to haul the rocks back to town, allowing for a family-friendly experience connecting with the heritage of St. George.','2023-10-09 23:05:30','2023-10-09 23:05:30'),(263,'trails',187,'short',NULL,NULL,'Summary: Hike Gates Lane and experience stunning rock formations and panoramic views in southern Utah.','2023-10-09 23:05:31','2023-10-09 23:05:31'),(264,'trails',188,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Desert Canyon Loop, a bike-friendly trail that is also open to pedestrians, offering mostly dirt singletrack and minimal rocky terrain to navigate, showcasing stunning desert vistas along the way.','2023-10-09 23:05:32','2023-10-09 23:05:32'),(265,'trails',189,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Black Brush trail and witness stunning views of the scenic desert landscape, towering sandstone cliffs, and abundant wildlife.','2023-10-09 23:05:32','2023-10-09 23:05:32'),(266,'trails',190,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Maverick trail to explore stunning red rock formations, towering sandstone cliffs, and breathtaking panoramic views of the surrounding landscape in southern Utah and northern Arizona.','2023-10-09 23:05:34','2023-10-09 23:05:34'),(267,'trails',191,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Stucki Springs loop on the Bearclaw Poppy trail network, where magnificent views of southern Utah\'s landscape await.','2023-10-09 23:05:35','2023-10-09 23:05:35'),(268,'trails',192,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Bearclaw Poppy Trail, a scenic route that links to various other trails, revealing stunning panoramic views of the surrounding landscapes in southern Utah and northern Arizona.','2023-10-09 23:05:36','2023-10-09 23:05:36'),(269,'trails',193,'short',NULL,NULL,'Kolob Arch Trail showcases the majestic and massive Kolob Arch, nestled high on a canyon wall in Zion National Park\'s remote Kolob Canyons District, providing hikers with stunning views of colorful canyon walls, soothing sounds of La Verkin Creek, wildlife encounters, and awe-inspiring vistas of the Kolob Terrace along its scenic seven-mile path.','2023-10-09 23:05:39','2023-10-09 23:05:39'),(270,'trails',194,'short',NULL,NULL,'Follow Deertrap Mountain Trail west, away from Cable Mountain Trail as it undulates in a serpentine manner to the rim of Zion Canyon, offering hikers a chance to see the Mountain of the Sun, Emerald Pool, The Twin Brothers, Angels Landing, The Court of the Patriarchs, and many more interesting points of interest along the way.','2023-10-09 23:05:41','2023-10-09 23:05:41'),(271,'trails',195,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hidden Canyon Trail, a moderately strenuous and exposed hike, follows along a cliff face to the mouth of a narrow canyon with long, steep drop-offs, requiring hikers to utilize chains bolted to the cliff for stability, and offering the option to scramble further into the hanging canyon for those with a controllable fear of heights.','2023-10-09 23:05:43','2023-10-09 23:05:43'),(272,'trails',196,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the breathtaking panoramic views of the canyon from Observation Point Trail, where clear views of Angel\'s Landing to the southwest and the Great White Throne to the due south will leave you in awe.','2023-10-09 23:05:44','2023-10-09 23:05:44'),(273,'trails',197,'short',NULL,NULL,'Weeping Rock Trail offers a family-friendly, somewhat steep, paved path that leads hikers to a stunning geologic oddity - a beautiful rock alcove.','2023-10-09 23:05:45','2023-10-09 23:05:45'),(274,'trails',198,'short',NULL,NULL,'Embark on the Watchman Trail, beginning from the Visitor Center, as it winds eastward and gradually becomes steep, leading hikers to the Watchman Trail mesa where they can witness breathtaking views of the Towers of the Virgin, lower Zion Canyon, and Springdale from a mesa overlooking the Visitor Center.','2023-10-09 23:05:47','2023-10-09 23:05:47'),(275,'trails',199,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore La Verkin Creek Trail, a 7-mile route starting at Lee Pass where you\'ll pass by nearby cliffs and Timber Creek, then descend to the creek bed for stunning views of the surrounding cliffs, before reaching Kolob Arch, one of the world\'s largest free-standing arches, and continue to Beartrap Canyon and Willis Canyon for additional day hiking options and extended trips.','2023-10-09 23:05:49','2023-10-09 23:05:49'),(276,'trails',200,'short',NULL,NULL,'Connect the Hop Valley Trail with Wildcat Canyon Trail on this low traffic trail, where hikers will enjoy the stunning beauty of Zion National Park.','2023-10-09 23:05:51','2023-10-09 23:05:51'),(277,'trails',201,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience sweeping views of the Northgate Peaks, wind through a ponderosa pine forest, and emerge onto the edge of Wildcat Canyon before climbing to connect with the West Rim Trail on the rolling backcountry Wildcat Canyon Trail. (Itinerary line item)','2023-10-09 23:05:52','2023-10-09 23:05:52'),(278,'trails',202,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Northgate Peaks Trail, a mostly flat trail that starts at a junction with Wildcat Canyon Trail and passes through open grasslands and pinyon-juniper, ending between the east and west Northgate Peaks where you\'ll enjoy great views of the North Guardian Angel, a large sandstone formation towering above the landscape; consider scrambling up either of the Northgate Peaks for an even better view, but remember to be respectful of the delicate desert fauna by sticking to defined paths.','2023-10-09 23:05:55','2023-10-09 23:05:55'),(279,'trails',203,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the somewhat unremarkable East Mesa Trail, which snakes through dense forest, providing great views of the Pink Cliffs, Brian Head Peak, Bulloch Gulch, and its surrounding canyon, with access to one of the best views in the park from Observation Point Trail.','2023-10-09 23:05:57','2023-10-09 23:05:57'),(280,'trails',204,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Hop Valley Trail, which meanders through open fields with wide open views of surrounding rock formations, leading you to the breathtaking Hop Valley with its flat sandy bottom and vertical walls, before descending into La Verkin Creek where you can access the impressive Kolob Arch and camp in designated sites.','2023-10-09 23:05:59','2023-10-09 23:05:59'),(281,'trails',205,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the East Rim Trail, beginning at the East Rim Trailhead, where you\'ll be guided towards the iconic sandstone cliffs of this region, with stunning views of Jolly Gulch, a breathtaking canyon that appears to plunge beneath you, making it an excellent spot to turn around for a shorter hike, before continuing through a picturesque ponderosa pine forest and reaching Stave Springs, a water source that may sometimes run dry, before descending sharply down 2,300 feet through Echo Canyon and the Observation Point Trail, ending at Weeping Rock in Zion Canyon.','2023-10-09 23:06:02','2023-10-09 23:06:02'),(282,'trails',206,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Cable Mountain Trail from East Rim Trail and Stave Spring, where you\'ll see juniper and pinyon pine forests and eventually pass Deertrap Mountain Trail, leading to a mellow descent to the edge of the canyon, offering views of Angel\'s Landing, The Great White Throne, and the historic Cable Mountain Draw Works, a 20th century aerial tramway used to transport lumber and timber from the mesas to the floor of Zion Canyon.','2023-10-09 23:06:05','2023-10-09 23:06:05'),(283,'trails',207,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Wrangler Trail, which takes you from Zion Lodge to the Court of the Patriarchs, offering scenic views of the Virgin River and the majestic Court of the Patriarchs rock formations.','2023-10-09 23:06:06','2023-10-09 23:06:06'),(284,'trails',208,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike along the mostly flat and well-maintained Kayenta Trail as it parallels the Virgin River to the south, offering scenic views.','2023-10-09 23:06:07','2023-10-09 23:06:07'),(285,'trails',209,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Parus Trail, a family-friendly adventure along a wide, paved path that heads northward from the visitor center in Zion National Park, where you will experience the constant natural symphony of the Virgin River as it flows through the canyon.','2023-10-09 23:06:09','2023-10-09 23:06:09'),(286,'trails',210,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the West Rim Trail, starting at the West Rim Trailhead near Lava Point, with sweeping views of Wildcat Canyon, Potato Hollow, and Right Fork Canyon, passing by Church Mesa, Inclined Temple, Ivins Mountain, and Greatheart Mesa, before reaching the southern terminus near the renowned Angel\'s Landing hike in Zion National Park.','2023-10-09 23:06:11','2023-10-09 23:06:11'),(287,'trails',211,'short',NULL,NULL,'Traverse the Telephone Canyon Trail, connecting Potato Hollow and Cabin Springs through an open meadow, providing a shortcut and a scenic view of the burn area, making it a great choice when there is snow on the rim, as it is quite exposed and likely to melt earlier.','2023-10-09 23:06:13','2023-10-09 23:06:13'),(288,'trails',212,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the exhilarating Angel\'s Landing Trail, accessed via the West Rim Trail, where hikers will encounter exposure, crowds, temperature changes, and the option to grab optional chains for assistance on the exposed sections, leading to a divine view at the summit, making it a truly unique and unrivaled adventure.','2023-10-09 23:06:14','2023-10-09 23:06:14'),(289,'trails',213,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore The Grotto Trail, a family-friendly hike that offers a quick walk with the opportunity to enjoy the sights and sounds of the Virgin River after a picnic, while also serving as a useful connector trail.','2023-10-09 23:06:16','2023-10-09 23:06:16'),(290,'trails',214,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Upper Emerald Pools Trail to see rocky outcrops, a sheer cliff, and connect to the middle and upper Emerald Pools.','2023-10-09 23:06:17','2023-10-09 23:06:17'),(291,'trails',215,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the family-friendly Lower Emerald Pools Trail and discover a well-maintained paved path with stunning sights of Zion\'s most cherished attractions to enjoy.','2023-10-09 23:06:18','2023-10-09 23:06:18'),(292,'trails',216,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike The Narrows - Wall Street to Big Springs Route for a challenging and exhilarating adventure through the narrowest part of the canyon, where 2000 foot walls surround you, passing by Floating Rock and Hiccup Springs before reaching the Boulder Pair and Sipping Turtle, ultimately ending at the picturesque Big Springs waterfall.','2023-10-09 23:06:20','2023-10-09 23:06:20'),(293,'trails',217,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike along an easy and flat trail by a creek, leading to a waterfall with deep waters for swimming during the summer on the Pine Creek Waterfall Trail.','2023-10-09 23:06:21','2023-10-09 23:06:21'),(294,'trails',218,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience a moderate hike up mostly sandstone rock and some sandy wash, where you will encounter dozens of small pools of water.','2023-10-09 23:06:22','2023-10-09 23:06:22'),(295,'trails',219,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike atop a massive landslide under The Sentinel on the Sand Bench Trail, a lollipop trail that traverses the remains of a massive ancient landslide, offering little shade and deep sand, so hikers should bring plenty of water and a can-do mentality, and be prepared for slightly slower forward progress.','2023-10-09 23:06:24','2023-10-09 23:06:24'),(296,'trails',220,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the breathtaking Left Fork (The Subway) trail, a permit-required area accessible from either direction, offering a classic, beautiful, and super fun bottom-up hike, providing an ideal experience for curious and adventurous souls to discover its wonders.','2023-10-09 23:06:26','2023-10-09 23:06:26'),(297,'trails',221,'short',NULL,NULL,'\"Ariel Point Trail leads to a rocky outcrop with an unobstructed viewpoint, offering the best of the nearby viewpoints, where hikers may need to find the route on the fading trail and follow the GPS track on their phone using the website\'s Hiking Project mobile app, passing through sparse trees and occasional meadows before reaching the trail\'s end at Ariel Point on the North Rim.\"','2023-10-09 23:06:28','2023-10-09 23:06:28'),(298,'trails',222,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Beamer Trail to the river and start up-canyon, where there are minor obstructions of Dox Sandstone at a couple of spots along the way, but overall, the route between Tanner Canyon and Palisades Creek is straightforward, with dense riparian vegetation near the shoreline, gradually giving way to rocky slopes and a dramatic change in the trail\'s character at Palisades Canyon, becoming a tedious, demanding trek along narrow, exposed ledges at the brink of high cliffs, and after climbing about 300 vertical feet up the talus north of Palisades, hikers will reach the top of the Tapeats Sandstone, where the trail continues all the way to the Little Colorado River, although hikers with a fear of heights may find this trail segment difficult due to its narrow and remarkably exposed portions, yet the trail is reasonably well-defined, and although there may be route finding problems when crossing the drainages, the main trail stays on the Tapeats rim for the entire hike, offering impressive views of the surrounding landscape.','2023-10-09 23:06:34','2023-10-09 23:06:34'),(299,'trails',223,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the challenging Bill Hall Trail, which starts with a one-mile ascent on the rim before descending steeply through formations and switchbacks, leading to the Esplanade and the junction with the trail from Indian Hollow, with an option to use a short rope for a 15-foot scramble. Hikers will see stunning views and experience the historical access point to the esplanade, honoring former park ranger Bill Hall.','2023-10-09 23:06:36','2023-10-09 23:06:36'),(300,'trails',224,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the breathtaking Clear Creek Trail, beginning approximately a mile north of Phantom Ranch on the North Kaibab Trail, as hikers climb through switchbacks to Phantom Overlook, offering a stunning view of Phantom Ranch below; continue on to traverse the Tonto Platform, crossing Sumner Wash and two minor drainages, witnessing the awe-inspiring Great Unconformity and the orange-colored Hakatai Shale, before reaching the dry tributary creek bed at the trail\'s end, where Brahma Temple comes into view.','2023-10-09 23:06:39','2023-10-09 23:06:39'),(301,'trails',225,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore Cliff Springs, a forested trail leading to a seeping cliffside spring near Cape Royal, where a chest-high boulder rests under a large overhang.','2023-10-09 23:06:41','2023-10-09 23:06:41'),(302,'trails',226,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the spectacularly fun Deer Creek trail, featuring parts of a slot canyon that can be carefully explored by older kids, while also offering stunning views of a waterfall and the historical attraction of huge outpourings of water since prehistoric times.','2023-10-09 23:06:42','2023-10-09 23:06:42'),(303,'trails',227,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Escalante Route from Tanner to Cardenas and encounter shallow gullies, a dry Cardenas Creek, and a climb up Dox Hill, with a fine view as the reward; then traverse west to the crest of the ridge north of Escalante Creek, being cautious to avoid missteps; continue down the canyon on a rising ramp of Shinumo Quartzite and follow the beach to Papago Creek, navigating ledges carefully; exit Papago onto tall ledges and climb to a horizontal trail above the river; traverse a short distance before descending a talus filled runnel back to the shoreline; and finally, decide whether to continue west on the East Tonto Trail or ascend to the rim on the New Hance Trail, as the Escalante Route is known for its challenging nature.','2023-10-09 23:06:47','2023-10-09 23:06:47'),(304,'trails',228,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the isolated former logging road, Francois Matthes Trail, which offers a unique perspective view of Zoroaster Temple and Wotans Throne, partially obscured by brush, and also presents a chance to see Cheyava Falls and the Clear Creek drainage, before retracing your steps back to the parking area.','2023-10-09 23:06:49','2023-10-09 23:06:49'),(305,'trails',229,'short',NULL,NULL,'Wind through the undulating forest and along the rim from Point Imperial to the North Kaibab Trailhead, where hikers will see scattered woods, meadows, a burned area, a canyon formed by Bright Angel Creek, and wonderful views down into the canyon over the rim, including upper Bright Angel Canyon, Mount Hayden, Marble Canyon, and Nankoweap Creek basin, with some route finding care and potential dense undergrowth and bushwhacking through the forested upper Walhalla plateau and past the Cape Royal Road.','2023-10-09 23:06:52','2023-10-09 23:06:52'),(306,'trails',230,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the seldom traveled former forest road of Komo Point Trail, gently and steadily descending southwards to the rim of the canyon, passing through numerous burned out trees from a huge wildfire, encountering downed trees and a faint trail that challenges route finding, and leading to an impressive overlook before retracing your steps back to the parking area.','2023-10-09 23:06:53','2023-10-09 23:06:53'),(307,'trails',231,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the remote North Bass Trail from Swamp Point, descending nearly a mile via switchbacks to Muav Saddle where you\'ll find an old patrol cabin, then continue contouring the base of the Coconino Sandstone for approximately a mile to the reliable Queen Anne Spring before descending to a benchmark in White Creek, crossing drainages along the Redwall rim and descending through the Redwall to White Creek, bypassing the pour-offs in the Bright Angel Shale and Tapeats Sandstone, and finally climbing to a saddle before descending to a large beach and terrace above the Colorado River.','2023-10-09 23:06:57','2023-10-09 23:06:57'),(308,'trails',232,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the North Kaibab trail, the second section of the AZT - Passage 38 - Grand Canyon - Inner Gorge, where hikers will descend steep switchbacks into a lush valley, pass through the distinctive Supai Tunnel, traverse the awe-inspiring Redwall Limestone cliffs, visit the historic Pumphouse Residence, explore the beautiful Ribbon Falls, and venture through the dangerous and scorching Inner Gorge of the Grand Canyon.','2023-10-09 23:06:59','2023-10-09 23:06:59'),(309,'trails',233,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the scenic Bridle Trail on the North Rim, where dogs and bikes are permitted and hikers will enjoy the beautiful surroundings.','2023-10-09 23:07:00','2023-10-09 23:07:00'),(310,'trails',234,'short',NULL,NULL,'Traverse the east side of the valley, switchback down into a dry streambed, and descend to Bright Angel Creek, passing shortfalls and keeping to the west bank of the creek, before crossing to the eastern side for a mile to reach the confluence with Roaring Springs, with possible cairns along the way, until you reach the intersection with the North Kaibab Trail at the Pumphouse seasonal ranger station and seasonal water source, but make sure to have good route finding skills due to social trails and dense undergrowth as the Old Bright Angel Trail was originally created in 1902 and has become overgrown.','2023-10-09 23:07:03','2023-10-09 23:07:03'),(311,'trails',235,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore Point Imperial, the highest of the North Rim overlooks, on a mostly flat trail starting from the north side of the Point Imperial overlook parking lot. Journey through open meadows and a burn area to witness the towering remnants of pine and aspen trees, left behind after the 2000 Outlook wildfire. Marvel at the regrowth of post-fire vegetation and the vibrant wildflowers in the early season. Enjoy scenic views of Marble Canyon and Boundary Ridge, including its highest point, Saddle Mountain, to the north. Traverse the increasingly hilly terrain until reaching a dirt turnaround and parking area, concluding this easy hike through the recovering Kaibab forest. Please note: some guidebooks may refer to this trail as a northern extension of the Ken Patrick Trail.','2023-10-09 23:07:08','2023-10-09 23:07:08'),(312,'trails',236,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Powell Plateau Trail, starting from Swamp Point and climbing switchbacks up to the plateau, where you\'ll explore a lovely forest of ponderosa pines and wildflowers, and if you\'re up for an adventurous challenge, persevere to Dutton Point for views of the Colorado River, all while experiencing the isolated feeling of this remote chunk of plateau land surrounded by the depths of the Grand Canyon.','2023-10-09 23:07:10','2023-10-09 23:07:10'),(313,'trails',237,'short',NULL,NULL,'Saddle Horse Loop provides amazing views of the Colorado River, accessible from the road between Toroweap Overlook and Tuweep Campground or from a trailhead near campsite 5 in the Tuweep Campground, with mostly level terrain and spectacular straight-down views of the river.','2023-10-09 23:07:12','2023-10-09 23:07:12'),(314,'trails',238,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike Saddle Mountain trail to the deep cut in the north rim, just below Saddle Mountain, passing through a ridgeline traverse, a wooded burn area, and flatter, parched ground, to reach the intersection with the Nankoweap Trail and enjoy great views along the way.','2023-10-09 23:07:14','2023-10-09 23:07:14'),(315,'trails',239,'short',NULL,NULL,'Adopt Schmutz Spring trail for a chance to see a stunning desert oasis.','2023-10-09 23:07:15','2023-10-09 23:07:15'),(316,'trails',240,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Thunder River Trail, crossing the expansive slickrock sections and descending through steep switchbacks to the cascades of Thunder River and Tapeats Creek, where hikers can rest and enjoy the designated Upper Tapeats campsite before continuing towards the Colorado River.','2023-10-09 23:07:17','2023-10-09 23:07:17'),(317,'trails',241,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Thunder River Trail south across the Esplanade, being alert for cairns that mark the route as it crosses slickrock sections and small drainages, before reaching a wonderful canyon view and descending steep switchbacks to the floor of Surprise Valley; continue east across the floor of Surprise Valley for about a mile before descending steep switchbacks to reach the cascades of Thunder River, a refreshing oasis in the desert.','2023-10-09 23:07:19','2023-10-09 23:07:19'),(318,'trails',242,'short',NULL,NULL,'Tiyo Point: Follow this increasingly overgrown former road through the encroaching forest, climbing over or around blown down trees, and keep an eye out for old trail markers nailed to trees, as you hike downhill on this infrequently used primitive trail, where wildflowers are your only company, until you reach the remote overlook with a single picnic area, offering peaceful isolation and a shady forest.','2023-10-09 23:07:22','2023-10-09 23:07:22'),(319,'trails',243,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience Tuweep\'s unique geology on the 3.1 mile Tuckup Trail, with two access points, one signed before reaching Tuweep Campground and the other leaving from campsite 10, explore one of the Grand Canyon\'s most challenging and least visited trails, offering an uncrowded, rustic, and remote experience with stunning views of the canyon.','2023-10-09 23:07:24','2023-10-09 23:07:24'),(320,'trails',244,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Tuckup to Tuweep Campground Connector trail and witness breathtaking views of the stunning landscape in southern Utah and northern Arizona.','2023-10-09 23:07:24','2023-10-09 23:07:24'),(321,'trails',245,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience a 0.8 mile loop trail at Unkar Delta, observing the ruins of many ancestral Puebloan dwellings and learning about its history as a farming community and the gradual growth and migration of its prehistoric people.','2023-10-09 23:07:26','2023-10-09 23:07:26'),(322,'trails',246,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike along the former forest road of Walhalla Glades, surrounded by the Grand Canyon on three sides and benefiting from a unique climate due to warm updrafts, to witness open fields, wooded areas, and a close approach to the canyon rim before reaching the southernmost point near Cape Royal and The Angels Window overlook area.','2023-10-09 23:07:28','2023-10-09 23:07:28'),(323,'trails',247,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Walhalla Connector to explore the east-west trail joining the Walhalla Glades, Francois Matthes, and Ariel Point trails, providing breathtaking views of the surrounding area.','2023-10-09 23:07:29','2023-10-09 23:07:29'),(324,'trails',248,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Widforss Point Trail, which follows the canyon rim for approximately 2.5 miles/4 kilometers, then ventures into a beautiful forest of conifers and aspens, emerging at Widforss Point, where hikers will witness stunning views of the Grand Canyon and the North Rim.','2023-10-09 23:07:31','2023-10-09 23:07:31'),(325,'trails',249,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the family-friendly Uncle Jim Trail, a pleasant and shaded loop trail with fantastic views of Bright Angel Canyon.','2023-10-09 23:07:32','2023-10-09 23:07:32'),(326,'trails',250,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike Lava Falls, a treacherous and intense trail with loose scree and shifting blocks of lava, requiring ice axes and good route finding skills, where hikers will see sandy beaches, a viewpoint above the famous Lava rapid, and possibly watch rafters navigating through, but caution should be taken as a false step can start a rock slide or cause a fall, while also noting the lack of shade or water, therefore proper planning and filtering river water for drinking is necessary, and utilizing the Hiking Project mobile app for navigation, ensuring current data and a charged device.','2023-10-09 23:07:36','2023-10-09 23:07:36'),(327,'trails',251,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the AZT - Passage 39 on the North Rim of the Grand Canyon, and discover breathtaking views of the awe-inspiring natural wonder.','2023-10-09 23:07:36','2023-10-09 23:07:36'),(328,'trails',252,'short',NULL,NULL,'Rainbow Rim Trail #10: Experience the remote and iconic singletrack trail that hugs the North Rim of the Grand Canyon, with options to set up camp at Locust Point and divide the trail into two separate out-and-back rides, offering stunning views of the area.','2023-10-09 23:07:38','2023-10-09 23:07:38'),(329,'trails',253,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Kaibab Plateau South on Passage 40 of the Arizona Trail, where stunning views of red rock canyons and majestic ponderosa pines await.','2023-10-09 23:07:39','2023-10-09 23:07:39'),(330,'trails',254,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the adventurous Granite Narrows Route, a well-traveled and well-marked path that connects Tapeats and Deer Creek, offering outstanding views and a unique perspective on the dramatic Granite Narrows section of the Colorado River.','2023-10-09 23:07:41','2023-10-09 23:07:41'),(331,'trails',255,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Granite Narrows High Route, a less direct but higher road through the narrows that avoids the exposed section of the lower trail, offering hikers panoramic views of the surrounding landscapes.','2023-10-09 23:07:42','2023-10-09 23:07:42'),(332,'trails',316,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the strenuous Peekaboo Loop, descending 670 feet from Bryce Point to a hoodoo-filled loop trail, encountering horses/mules along the way, before ascending back up to Bryce Point, with pit toilets available in summer months and a recommended clockwise direction.','2023-10-09 23:07:44','2023-10-09 23:07:44'),(333,'trails',317,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the iconic Navajo Loop Trail, beginning and ending at Sunset Point, as you wind through switchbacks between narrow walls of colorful limestone, offering breathtaking views of towering Douglas-fir trees and the famous Thor\'s hammer hoodoo.','2023-10-09 23:07:45','2023-10-09 23:07:45'),(334,'trails',318,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike from Sunset Point to Sunrise Point on the Rim Trail where hikers will enjoy an easy 1-mile walk along the paved section of the trail with views of the iconic Bryce Canyon Amphitheater and the hoodoos below.','2023-10-09 23:07:47','2023-10-09 23:07:47'),(335,'trails',319,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Bryce Amphitheater, the park\'s most renowned section, boasting the highest concentration of irregular rock spires, known as \"hoodoos\" on Earth, with accessible viewpoints like Bryce Point, Inspiration Point, Sunset Point, and Sunrise Point offering year-round panoramic vistas and serving as trailheads for hikes along the rim of the Paunsaugunt plateau; prioritize these must-see viewpoints if you have limited time in the park.','2023-10-09 23:07:49','2023-10-09 23:07:49'),(336,'trails',320,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Mossy Cave Trail, one of the only hikes in the park that starts with a climb and ends with a descent, located off Highway 12 in the park\'s northern end; see delicate rocks and sensitive plants, and enjoy scenic views, including access to the water.','2023-10-09 23:07:51','2023-10-09 23:07:51'),(337,'trails',321,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Rim Trail from either Sunrise or Sunset Point, an easy 0.5-mile (0.8 km) walk with a 34-foot (10-meter) elevation gain, providing spectacular views of the park\'s most popular area and accessible for pets on paved surfaces.','2023-10-09 23:07:53','2023-10-09 23:07:53'),(338,'trails',322,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Bristlecone Loop Trail, meandering through the forest atop the park\'s highest portion, reaching elevations over 9,100 feet, passing by ancient bristlecone pines and offering vistas of the Four Corners area, while enjoying sightings of various wildlife including grouse, woodpeckers, owls, squirrels, chipmunks, Ravens, and Steller\'s Jays.','2023-10-09 23:07:56','2023-10-09 23:07:56'),(339,'trails',323,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the splendor of hiking amongst the hoodoos on the least difficult trail descending from the rim into the Bryce Canyon Amphitheater, the Queen\'s Garden Trail, where hikers will see the Queen Victoria hoodoo at the end of a short spur trail.','2023-10-09 23:07:57','2023-10-09 23:07:57'),(340,'trails',324,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Queen\'s/Navajo Combination Loop, descending into the amphitheater at Sunrise Point via the Queen\'s Garden trail, connecting either with the Two Bridges or Wall Street side of the Navajo Loop to ascend to Sunset Point, and complete the loop by walking the Rim Trail back to Sunrise Point, where hikers will enjoy open views, unique hoodoos, iconic switchbacks, towering canyon walls, and stunning views into the Bryce Canyon Amphitheater below.','2023-10-09 23:08:00','2023-10-09 23:08:00'),(341,'trails',325,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore Tower Bridge on the southern portion of the Fairyland Loop trail, a moderate 3.0-mile roundtrip hike that descends to a shady spur trail leading to the Tower Bridge hoodoo, offering hikers the opportunity to return the way they came or continue on to complete the entirety of the Fairyland Loop Trail, with parking available at Sunrise Point.','2023-10-09 23:08:02','2023-10-09 23:08:02'),(342,'trails',326,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the rugged Swamp Canyon Loop to experience a more intimate connection with the landscape from the sheltered viewpoint, and explore the forested backcountry with less signage and a map recommended, seeing the smaller, more secluded Swamp Canyon, instead of the grander viewpoints in the park.','2023-10-09 23:08:04','2023-10-09 23:08:04'),(343,'trails',327,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the quiet 8-mile Fairyland Loop hike in southern Utah, starting either at Fairyland Point or at the Tower Bridge trailhead north of Sunrise Point, where hikers will be treated to long views of the unique hoodoos in the Bryce Amphitheater surrounded by the breathtaking beauty of the canyon.','2023-10-09 23:08:06','2023-10-09 23:08:06'),(344,'trails',328,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Navajo/Peekaboo Combination Loop, a strenuous 4.9 mile (7.9 km) hike with a rapid elevation gain of 1475 feet (450 meters), starting at Sunset Point and descending down either side of the Navajo Loop to connect with the Peekaboo Loop, where hikers will encounter horses and mules and should give them the right-of-way, before ascending back to Sunset Point, taking approximately 3-4 hours.','2023-10-09 23:08:10','2023-10-09 23:08:10'),(345,'trails',329,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Bryce Amphitheater Traverse, combining the Peekaboo Loop with the Queen\'s Garden trail, descending from Bryce Point, turning left on Peekaboo Loop, connecting to Queen\'s Garden trail to ascend to Sunrise Point, encountering horses and mules along the way.','2023-10-09 23:08:12','2023-10-09 23:08:12'),(346,'trails',330,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the ultimate hike by combining the Queen\'s Garden, Navajo Loop, and Peekaboo Loop amphitheater hikes into the Figure-8 Combination where hikers will see stunning rock formations, descend into Queen\'s Garden, connect with the Peekaboo Loop in a clockwise direction, ascend the Navajo Loop (Two Bridges in winter), and complete the trail by hiking back to Sunrise Point along the Rim Trail.','2023-10-09 23:08:15','2023-10-09 23:08:15'),(347,'trails',331,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Riggs Spring Loop, a strenuous 8.6-mile trail that takes you through spruce, fir, and bristlecone forests, offering beautiful views of distant cliffs, starting and ending at Yovimpa Point where accessible restrooms are available (year-round) but no water-fill stations; permits are required for overnight stays in designated backcountry campsites, with the option to combine this loop with additional nights along the Under-the-Rim Trail.','2023-10-09 23:08:17','2023-10-09 23:08:17'),(348,'trails',332,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience Bryce Canyon at your own pace by riding your bike, start at the Park Shuttle Station and follow the Shared-Use Path for 2.4 miles to the Visitor Center, then continue for 0.8 miles to the Sunrise Point General Store, and finally ride 1.8 miles to Inspiration Point, ascending and descending various elevations, while enjoying scenic viewpoints, paved surfaces, and following rules and regulations for cyclists.','2023-10-09 23:08:20','2023-10-09 23:08:20'),(349,'trails',333,'short',NULL,NULL,'Attend the Night Sky Telescope Program, available through August 4, 2023, and experience the dark sky of Bryce Canyon with guided tours and telescopes, learning about the science and cultural history of this International Dark Sky Park.','2023-10-09 23:08:22','2023-10-09 23:08:22'),(350,'trails',334,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience Bryce Canyon\'s dark skies and pristine night views with thousands of stars and the Milky Way Galaxy shooting across the sky; don\'t miss the opportunity for night photography on the Queen\'s Garden or Navajo Trails.','2023-10-09 23:08:23','2023-10-09 23:08:23'),(351,'trails',335,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Under-the-Rim Trail from Rainbow Point to Bryce Point, enjoying stunning views of distant cliffs, forests, meadows, and solitude, and make sure to explore the six campsites and one group site along the trail for a two-night adventure.','2023-10-09 23:08:25','2023-10-09 23:08:25'),(352,'trails',336,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the enchantment of the hoodoos, the history of the people, and the unique ecosystems of Bryce Canyon National Park on a one-hour Rim Walk with a ranger, starting at Sunset Point near the observation platform, covering about 1/2 mile on a paved path, suitable for all ages, offered typically Memorial Day through Labor Day at 2:00 p.m. at Sunset Point, learn about the park and enjoy nature while spending an hour on this wheelchair accessible walk.','2023-10-09 23:08:28','2023-10-09 23:08:28'),(353,'trails',337,'short',NULL,NULL,'Participants must arrive at the Visitor Center a few minutes before 4 p.m. to be included in the Full Moon Hike lottery, and if their ticket is drawn, they will embark on a 1 to 2-mile ranger\'s choice hike that can range from easy along the rim of the plateau to steep and rocky trails descending into the canyon, providing a unique experience to witness the hoodoos take on spooky personalities under the moonlight.','2023-10-09 23:08:30','2023-10-09 23:08:30'),(354,'trails',338,'short',NULL,NULL,'Attend an engaging evening program at Bryce Canyon National Park\'s historic Bryce Canyon Lodge auditorium at 8:30 p.m., where a ranger will explore topics such as navigation, the effects of nature on health, moonlight, thunderstorms, geology, scary stories, adaptations, and more, showcasing the park\'s unique resources and compelling stories.','2023-10-09 23:08:32','2023-10-09 23:08:32'),(355,'trails',339,'short',NULL,NULL,'Attend a Hoodoo Geology Talk at Sunset Point to learn about the evolution of the magical hoodoos and the groups of people who have passed through Bryce Canyon\'s amphitheaters along the rim.','2023-10-09 23:08:34','2023-10-09 23:08:34'),(356,'trails',340,'short',NULL,NULL,'Join a ranger-led snowshoe hike to experience the stark white of freshly fallen snow, red rocks, blue sky, and evergreen trees in the magical winter season of Bryce Canyon, where the scenery changes dramatically at 8,000 feet, with snowshoes and poles provided for all levels of experience.','2023-10-09 23:08:36','2023-10-09 23:08:36'),(357,'trails',341,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Southern Scenic Drive and have a picnic at the CCC Picnic Area located at mile 4.75, where hikers will enjoy stunning views of the surrounding landscape while following federal laws not to approach dangerous wildlife.','2023-10-09 23:08:37','2023-10-09 23:08:37'),(358,'trails',342,'short',NULL,NULL,'Bryce Point serves as a trailhead for accessing Hat Shop Peekaboo Loop, Hat Shop, and the Under-the-Rim trail, offering hikers a strenuous out-and-back hike with stunning views of the colorful cliffs of the Grand Staircase, as they descend 1,380 feet (421 meters) over two miles to a cluster of balanced-rock hoodoos wearing distinct \"hats\" of large gray boulders.','2023-10-09 23:08:40','2023-10-09 23:08:40'),(359,'trails',343,'short',NULL,NULL,'Drive the Southern Scenic Drive and see 9 unique viewpoints along the southern stretches of the main park road, overlooking geologic features and the grand landscape of Paria Valley and the Grand Staircase.','2023-10-09 23:08:41','2023-10-09 23:08:41'),(360,'trails',344,'short',NULL,NULL,'Become a Junior Ranger at Bryce Canyon National Park by exploring the park, attending a ranger presentation or hike, completing activities in an age-appropriate booklet, and returning to the Visitor Center to be inducted as a Junior Ranger and receive a free badge and other souvenirs.','2023-10-09 23:08:43','2023-10-09 23:08:43'),(361,'trails',345,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the solitude of Bryce\'s forested backcountry on the 22.9 mile Under-the-Rim Trail, which meanders through forests and meadows, offering stunning views of rainbow-hued cliffs in the distance, and permits backcountry camping only in designated campsites.','2023-10-09 23:08:44','2023-10-09 23:08:44'),(362,'trails',346,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Park Film, \"A Song of Seasons,\" playing in the Visitor Center theater every half hour when the Visitor Center is open, showcasing stunning visuals and captivating narration of the natural wonders found in the area, with online viewing options available that include captions and assisted listening devices upon request.','2023-10-09 23:08:46','2023-10-09 23:08:46'),(363,'trails',347,'short',NULL,NULL,'On a day hike in this high elevation park, hikers can enjoy the beauty of Mossy Cave, a 0.8 mi (1.3 km) easy hike, the stunning views along Sunset to Sunrise, a 1.0 mi (1.6 km) easy hike along the Rim Trail, and the unique Bristlecone Loop, a 1.0 mi (1.6 km) easy hike, among other beautiful trails.','2023-10-09 23:08:49','2023-10-09 23:08:49'),(364,'trails',348,'short',NULL,NULL,'Collect your Bryce Canyon cancellation stamp at the passport counter, located in the back right corner of the Visitor Center, and commemorate your visit with a unique addition to your passport book.','2023-10-09 23:08:50','2023-10-09 23:08:50'),(365,'trails',349,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Hoodoos and discover the nine \"Hike the Hoodoos!\" benchmarks, located along eight different hiking trails in Bryce Canyon National Park, and earn a special reward by hiking at least 3 miles and finding the benchmark survey markers along the way.','2023-10-09 23:08:52','2023-10-09 23:08:52'),(366,'trails',350,'short',NULL,NULL,'Observe wildlife while hiking in Bryce Canyon National Park, where you can spot many animals including migratory hummingbirds, nesting Peregrine Falcons, Rocky Mountain Elk, and Pronghorn that frequent the canyons, plateau, and forested areas of the park.','2023-10-09 23:08:53','2023-10-09 23:08:53'),(367,'trails',351,'short',NULL,NULL,'Observe over 175 different species of birds while birdwatching in Bryce Canyon National Park, including western bluebirds, American robins, hummingbirds, hermit thrushes, and Townsend\'s solitaires, as well as other species along the Southern Scenic Drive, Bryce Amphitheater, Mossy Cave, Tropic Trail, and outside the park in Bryce Canyon City.','2023-10-09 23:08:56','2023-10-09 23:08:56'),(368,'trails',352,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience snowshoeing in Bryce Canyon National Park as you explore the Rim Trail, Bristlecone Loop, Fairyland Road, and Paria Road, enjoying the breathtaking winter scenery and the opportunity to join a ranger-led snowshoe hike for free with provided snowshoes and poles.','2023-10-09 23:08:58','2023-10-09 23:08:58'),(369,'trails',353,'short',NULL,NULL,'Watch the Native Perspectives video in the Human History area of the Visitor Center Museum to gain insight into the area from a Native perspective.','2023-10-09 23:08:59','2023-10-09 23:08:59'),(370,'trails',354,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the scenic Rim Trail, connecting Bryce Point, Inspiration Point, Sunset Point, Sunrise Point, and Fairyland Point, offering hikers a relatively easy hike with spectacular views of the park\'s most popular area; for a very easy walk, we recommend hiking from one point and back along the 0.5 mile (0.8 km) section between Sunset and Sunrise Points, with an elevation gain of 34 feet (10 meters).','2023-10-09 23:09:01','2023-10-09 23:09:01'),(371,'trails',355,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the South Rim Trail and see unique perspectives of hoodoos, arches, waterfalls, and more as the trail winds along the edge of the amphitheater at Cedar Breaks National Monument.','2023-10-09 23:09:03','2023-10-09 23:09:03'),(372,'trails',357,'short',NULL,NULL,'Travel Glen Canyon’s Moonscape Backcountry on the way to Alstrom Point, a 46-mile long drive through the southwest region of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, offering adventurous explorers views of Tropic Shale, Romana Sandstone, and ever-changing canyons of Lake Powell, with side hikes at Wiregrass Canyon and Resurrection Canyon, side drives at Crosby Canyon, and numerous side roads leading to Escalante UT.','2023-10-09 23:09:05','2023-10-09 23:09:05'),(373,'trails',358,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience a day trip or backpack hike to Coyote Gulch, a tributary of the Escalante River, with multiple hiking entry points located off of the Hole-in-the-Rock Road in Escalante, Utah. Depending on your chosen point of entry into the canyon, you will encounter arches, a natural bridge, waterfalls, and a perennial stream that will require walking through or crossing multiple times. Ensure you check road conditions before your trip and be aware of the potential for flash floods in canyon country. Group size is limited to 12 people, and it is crucial to practice Leave No Trace principles and properly dispose of human waste using a portable toilet or specifically engineered bag system. Fires are not allowed, and building rock cairns is discouraged.','2023-10-09 23:09:09','2023-10-09 23:09:09'),(374,'trails',360,'short',NULL,NULL,'Embark on a hike from your boat to the observation at Rainbow Bridge, where you will witness the majestic natural bridge standing 290 feet tall and spanning 275 feet across the stream, a symbol of resilience and hope for the Navajo people, with the opportunity to see the hanging garden, the massive Navajo Mountain, and the commemorative plaques honoring Nasja Begay and Jim Mike for their assistance in the discovery of Rainbow Bridge in 1909.','2023-10-09 23:09:12','2023-10-09 23:09:12'),(375,'trails',361,'short',NULL,NULL,'Scramble down Cathedral Wash Trail to witness canyon walls rising as you journey deeper through Kaibab Limestone and Toroweap Formations, ultimately reaching the Colorado River where Cathedral Wash Rapid is formed by canyon debris.','2023-10-09 23:09:14','2023-10-09 23:09:14'),(376,'trails',362,'short',NULL,NULL,'Visit Lonely Dell Ranch Historic Site in Lees Ferry, accessed via Hwy 89 south and Hwy 89A west from Page or via Hwy 89A and Hwy 67 from the North Rim of the Grand Canyon, where a self-guided walking tour leads you through the orchard, log cabins, stone ranch house, and pioneer cemetery, offering a glimpse into the isolated settlement\'s history and the daily life of the families who lived there during the Great Mormon Migration. While exploring the mixed terrain of gravel, sandy, and rocky surfaces, you can also enjoy the shade of the trees and even collect fruit from the orchard for personal consumption, with a limit of 5 gallons per person per day.','2023-10-09 23:09:18','2023-10-09 23:09:18'),(377,'trails',363,'short',NULL,NULL,'Contemplate your place in the landscape at the big relief map in the corner of Glen Canyon Conservancy\'s flagship store, where you can explore the precisely accurate topography of the Glen Canyon National Recreation Area and surrounding vicinity, including 12 natural bridges and arches hidden within, providing a unique opportunity for wayfinding and mind wandering.','2023-10-09 23:09:20','2023-10-09 23:09:20'),(378,'trails',364,'short',NULL,NULL,'Camp on Lake Powell\'s undeveloped shoreline, allowing you to beach your boat for day trips or camping, and enjoy the deep turquoise waters and stunning views of the stars at night, while adhering to safety tips, regulations, and environmental guidelines.','2023-10-09 23:09:21','2023-10-09 23:09:21'),(379,'trails',365,'short',NULL,NULL,'Stroll to Horseshoe Bend Overlook, featured as a landmark for Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, Grand Canyon National Park, the Cities of Page AZ, Kanab UT, St George UT, and the Navajo Nation, where you can take in the magnificent view of Horseshoe Bend and the Colorado River, encompassing a unique mix of jurisdictions and managed under the regulations of the Americans with Disabilities Act, while being prepared with plenty of water, sturdy footwear, sunscreen, and other basic hiking essentials, and avoiding the hottest part of the day.','2023-10-09 23:09:25','2023-10-09 23:09:25'),(380,'trails',367,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the scenic beauty of Hermit Road, a 7-mile (11 km) route along the west end of Grand Canyon Village on the South Rim, offering breathtaking views of the Grand Canyon, including the Colorado River at Hopi Point and the crashing sound of the river at Granite Rapid from Pima Point. Accessible by shuttle bus, foot, bicycle, or commercial bus tour, this popular road features nine designated viewpoints and the opportunity to enjoy short or long walks along the paved and dirt trails of the 7.8-mile (12.6 km) Canyon Rim Trail alongside the road. Private vehicles are only allowed during the winter months of December, January, and February, with shuttle bus service available during the rest of the year.','2023-10-09 23:09:29','2023-10-09 23:09:29'),(381,'trails',368,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Weeping Rock Trailhead at Shuttle Stop 7 and see small trailside exhibits, minor drop-offs, and a rock alcove with dripping springs at the end, with pit toilets available at the parking area.','2023-10-09 23:09:30','2023-10-09 23:09:30'),(382,'trails',369,'short',NULL,NULL,'Attend ranger-led activities to discover what makes Zion National Park special, with topics including geology, wildlife, art, human history, and more, offering a chance to learn and explore the park\'s diverse features.','2023-10-09 23:09:32','2023-10-09 23:09:32'),(383,'trails',370,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Upper Emerald Pools Trail, a moderate 1 mile (1.6 km) trail that can be accessed via the Kayenta Trail, Lower Emerald Pools Trail, or Middle Emerald Pools Trail, to see majestic seasonal waterfalls, towering canyon walls, and canyon landmarks such as Lady Mountain and The Great White Throne, with the trail ending at the sandy shore of Upper Emerald Pool.','2023-10-09 23:09:35','2023-10-09 23:09:35'),(384,'trails',371,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the 7.6 mile roundtrip Sand Bench Trail to explore the remains of an ancient rockfall, follow the Virgin River, and ascend a landslide that dammed the river 8,000 years ago, creating a lake that extended to Angels Landing, with access from the Zion Lodge or Court of the Patriarchs shuttle stops, with little shade and possible ice patches in winter, and water and restrooms available at The Zion Lodge.','2023-10-09 23:09:37','2023-10-09 23:09:37'),(385,'trails',372,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike Lower Emerald Pool Trail near Zion Lodge to see the Lower Emerald Pool and ephemeral waterfalls while passing through pinyon-juniper woodland, with access to other Emerald Pools trails and the Kayenta Trail, and amenities including restrooms and water filling stations at Zion Lodge.','2023-10-09 23:09:39','2023-10-09 23:09:39'),(386,'trails',373,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Middle Emerald Pools Trail, a moderate 2.2 mi (3.5 km) trail that offers expansive views of main Zion Canyon, shaded pinyon-juniper woodlands, and emerald-colored pools of water above small waterfalls.','2023-10-09 23:09:41','2023-10-09 23:09:41'),(387,'trails',374,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike to Angels Landing from The Grotto (shuttle stop #6), crossing the road and bridge to the sandy West Rim Trail, then along the challenging trail with uneven steps and chains intermittently anchored along the route, navigating a narrow trail with exposed edges and sheer drop-offs, yielding to others on the trail, and resting at the end to enjoy the spectacular views of Zion Canyon, with restrooms and water filling stations available at Scout Lookout and The Grotto.','2023-10-09 23:09:43','2023-10-09 23:09:43'),(388,'trails',375,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the strenuous West Rim Trail that ascends 1,488 ft (454 m) to Scout Lookout, where hikers can enjoy views of Zion Canyon and look for endangered California condors, before continuing to Angels Landing (permit required).','2023-10-09 23:09:45','2023-10-09 23:09:45'),(389,'trails',376,'short',NULL,NULL,'Kayenta Trail provides hikers with panoramic views of Zion Canyon as they follow the Virgin River, ascend to the Lower and Upper Emerald Pool Trails, and have the option to return on the same trail or make a loop using one of the Emerald Pools trails, while passing by long drop-offs; water and restrooms are available at The Grotto.','2023-10-09 23:09:47','2023-10-09 23:09:47'),(390,'trails',377,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore Zion\'s East Side on the moderate 1-mile Canyon Overlook Trail, featuring sandstone steps and a metal handrail, leading to breathtaking views of red and cream-colored slickrock, the stunning Towers of the Virgin, and the possibility of spotting bighorn sheep; limited parking, multiple attempts may be needed, pit toilets available.','2023-10-09 23:09:49','2023-10-09 23:09:49'),(391,'trails',378,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike The Narrows and witness the Virgin River winding through a narrow slot canyon, requiring wading, walking, and sometimes swimming in the river with the constant dangers of flash flooding and hypothermia.','2023-10-09 23:09:51','2023-10-09 23:09:51'),(392,'trails',379,'short',NULL,NULL,'Visit Riverside Walk, a flat and paved 2.2 mile round-trip trail in Zion Canyon, where hikers will witness the narrowing of sheer sandstone walls as they follow the Virgin River, passing exhibits along the way to learn about canyon carving, erosion, and the ecological richness of the area, with the option to continue to The Narrows or turn back at the end of the paved trail, while having access to water and restrooms at the Temple of Sinawava.','2023-10-09 23:09:54','2023-10-09 23:09:54'),(393,'trails',380,'short',NULL,NULL,'See the Kolob Arch, one of the world\'s largest natural arches, on the La Verkin Creek Trail to Kolob Arch starting at Lee Pass Trailhead along the Kolob Canyons Road with stunning views of the Kolob Canyons and a seven-mile descent toward La Verkin Creek.','2023-10-09 23:09:56','2023-10-09 23:09:56'),(394,'trails',381,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike, bike, or walk a dog along the paved Pa\'rus Trail, which follows the Virgin River and offers wildlife sightings, multiple river access points, trailside exhibits, and stunning views of the Watchman, making it a great spot for sunset viewing, stargazing, and astrophotography.','2023-10-09 23:09:58','2023-10-09 23:09:58'),(395,'trails',382,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the beauty of Timber Creek Overlook Trail, an easy hike with stunning views of Shuntavi Butte, Timber Creek, Kolob Terrace, Pine Valley Mountains, and possibly even Mount Trumbull, located 100 miles away at the north rim of the Grand Canyon, while enjoying a display of desert wildflowers in the spring and early summer months.','2023-10-09 23:10:00','2023-10-09 23:10:00'),(396,'trails',383,'short',NULL,NULL,'Discover Climbing in Zion National Park: Explore the 2,000-foot sandstone cliffs, known worldwide for their big wall climbs and adventurous nature, where most routes are not recommended for inexperienced climbers due to the difficulty and soft nature of the rock, while bolted routes are often sparsely protected.','2023-10-09 23:10:02','2023-10-09 23:10:02'),(397,'trails',384,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Taylor Creek Trail and discover its stunning natural formations, including the Double Arch Alcove, as you hike through a narrow box canyon, crossing the Taylor Creek and passing the Kanarraville Fold and historic homestead cabins.','2023-10-09 23:10:03','2023-10-09 23:10:03'),(398,'trails',385,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the easy 1.0 mi (1.6 km) Grotto Trail connecting shuttle stop #5 at the Zion Lodge to shuttle stop #6 at The Grotto, a leisure walk through shaded terrain where you may spot wildlife like birds, turkey, and deer, with restrooms available at both locations.','2023-10-09 23:10:05','2023-10-09 23:10:05'),(399,'trails',386,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the moderate Watchman Trail, accessible from the Zion Canyon Visitor Center, offering a scenic view of the Watchman, Temples and Towers, lower Zion Canyon, and the Town of Springdale as it climbs 368 ft (112 m) through sandy, rocky, and uneven terrain with moderate drop-offs; restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Visitor Center, while bikes are not permitted.','2023-10-09 23:10:07','2023-10-09 23:10:07'),(400,'trails',387,'short',NULL,NULL,'Spencer Trail: Ascend through tricky rock and scree, traverse exposed cliffs, thread through boulders, and climb sandy stone steps to reach the plateau; at the top, experience stunning views of Page, Paria River, Lake Powell, Glen Canyon, Echo Peaks, Vermilion Cliffs, Navajo Mountain, and Lee\'s Ferry, while also observing historical mining relics and the sunken wreck of a paddle boat.','2023-10-09 23:10:10','2023-10-09 23:10:10'),(401,'trails',388,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike along an easy and sandy trail to witness amazing views of a natural wonder at Horseshoe Bend Trail.','2023-10-09 23:10:11','2023-10-09 23:10:11'),(402,'trails',389,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Antelope Canyon Trail to witness the breathtaking beauty and unique rock formations of the stunning slot canyon.','2023-10-09 23:10:12','2023-10-09 23:10:12'),(403,'trails',8,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Paradise Canyon Trail, a picturesque hike that begins and concludes at the Dark Hollow Trail #32032, offering breathtaking views along the way.','2023-10-09 23:11:56','2023-10-09 23:11:56'),(404,'trails',11,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike through a colorful canyon of painted rocks, climb a ridge for stunning views, and reach a high viewpoint near Noah\'s Arc, showcasing excellent vistas of the red and yellow painted canyon.','2023-10-09 23:11:58','2023-10-09 23:11:58'),(405,'trails',12,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Vermillion Castle Trail, starting from the signed parking, for a determined climb and then a level walk along a large, rocky edge, where you will pass pillars of composite rocks, bright yellow hoodoos, and the deep reds of the Vermillion Castle, with extensive views of the canyon at the end, followed by a short, steep climb to a viewpoint below Vermillion Castle.','2023-10-09 23:12:00','2023-10-09 23:12:00'),(406,'trails',13,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Hidden Haven Falls Trail, a family-friendly hike with a generally flat and shaded trail that leads to a beautiful waterfall where caution is needed.','2023-10-09 23:12:01','2023-10-09 23:12:01'),(407,'trails',14,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Lowder Ponds Trail #16 to see scenic ponds surrounded by lush vegetation and enjoy the peaceful ambiance of this picturesque natural setting.','2023-10-09 23:12:02','2023-10-09 23:12:02'),(408,'trails',16,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Tropic Valley Connector, a family-friendly trail that offers an easy, wide doubletrack, avoiding the steep climb and descent from the rim, leading hikers to picturesque hoodoos and the beautiful canyon floor without the need to climb back to the rim.','2023-10-09 23:12:04','2023-10-09 23:12:04'),(409,'trails',17,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the stunning Fairyland Loop Trail, a challenging 8-mile hike that showcases the impressive Fairyland amphitheater of hoodoos, the Sinking Ship formation, the mesmerizing Tower Bridge, the parched Campbell Canyon, the Chinese Wall formation, and breathtaking vistas of towering cliffs along the way.','2023-10-09 23:12:06','2023-10-09 23:12:06'),(410,'trails',18,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the stunning Peek-A-Boo Loop Trail, known for its steep, roller coaster-like terrain and breathtaking views of colorful hoodoos and natural rock arches such as the Wall of Windows, Hindu Temples, and the Cathedral, making it a must-visit destination for adventurous travelers.','2023-10-09 23:12:08','2023-10-09 23:12:08'),(411,'trails',19,'short',NULL,NULL,'Peek-A-Boo Loop Connector: Start from Bryce Point parking area, switchback steeply down, cross cliff bands, contour below rock walls, cross ridges and bluffs, losing elevation, keep an eye out for named hoodoo rock formations, end at southern tip of Peek-A-Boo Loop - prepare for stunning sights and a challenging climb out of the canyon on the 3-mile Peek-A-Boo Loop Trail.','2023-10-09 23:12:11','2023-10-09 23:12:11'),(412,'trails',20,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the family-friendly Queens Garden Trail to see man-made tunnels, numerous hoodoos, spires, and the Queen Victoria formation, while encountering some steep sections along the way.','2023-10-09 23:12:12','2023-10-09 23:12:12'),(413,'trails',24,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the lengthy Sheep Creek Trail as an exit route from the park or a fee-free entrance into the park, following Sheep Creek wash along the canyon bottom, passing landmarks like Swamp Canyon, the Sheep Creek backcountry campsite, and Short Canyon, until reaching the Sheep Creek Trailhead and parking area at the end of Sheep Creek Flat Road, just inside the BLM land boundary.','2023-10-09 23:12:14','2023-10-09 23:12:14'),(414,'trails',27,'short',NULL,NULL,'Descend the Whiteman Connecting Trail to access the Under-the-Rim Trail and Swamp Canyon camp, where you can observe breathtaking views and camping opportunities.','2023-10-09 23:12:15','2023-10-09 23:12:15'),(415,'trails',29,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the family-friendly Mossy Cave Trail to see ample water features and interesting rock formations, learning about how Mormon settlers diverted the creek here while following a popular scenic path that leads to a small waterfall cascading over a ledge.','2023-10-09 23:12:17','2023-10-09 23:12:17'),(416,'trails',31,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the family-friendly Shakespeare Arch on the Sentinel Trail, where breathtaking views of Utah\'s stunning landscape in every direction await.','2023-10-09 23:12:18','2023-10-09 23:12:18'),(417,'trails',38,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the smooth and gently sloping Highlands Trail, which offers stunning vistas and serves as a pleasant connector between the Greens Lake Trailhead and C-Trail, opening up in 2017.','2023-10-09 23:12:19','2023-10-09 23:12:19'),(418,'trails',46,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Buckhorn Trail #33037, starting from the Red Canyon Campground, and climb steadily through scenic red rock terrain to a vantage point overlooking Red Canyon, with a junction to the Golden Wall Trail at approximately 3/4 of a mile into the hike.','2023-10-09 23:12:21','2023-10-09 23:12:21'),(419,'trails',55,'short',NULL,NULL,'Oak Grove Trail, a steep trail that quickly transitions between the desert and a lush pine forest, offers breathtaking views of the Colorado Plateau and red rock formations in the distance, as well as impressive granite cliffs and boulders within the Pine Valley Mountains, before descending into a shaded evergreen forest and meeting up with the Summit Trail.','2023-10-09 23:12:23','2023-10-09 23:12:23'),(420,'trails',62,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience a variety of terrains on the Race Course Loop, including sand, climbs, downhill, and rugged sections, offering breathtaking sights along the way.','2023-10-09 23:12:24','2023-10-09 23:12:24'),(421,'trails',65,'short',NULL,NULL,'Experience the Earl Trail\'s steep incline/decline on the south end while witnessing spectacular scenery.','2023-10-09 23:12:25','2023-10-09 23:12:25'),(422,'trails',68,'short',NULL,NULL,'Start at the practice loop to experience the perfect starting point and witness stunning natural views in southern Utah and northern Arizona.','2023-10-09 23:12:26','2023-10-09 23:12:26'),(423,'trails',70,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Outlaw Trail, a thrilling optional connection on the Race Course Loop, revealing breathtaking views of the majestic southern Utah and northern Arizona landscapes.','2023-10-09 23:12:27','2023-10-09 23:12:27'),(424,'trails',84,'short',NULL,NULL,'Explore the Willis Creek Narrows Trail, navigate an easy-to-follow slot canyon, and witness stunning scenery along the mostly flat path.','2023-10-09 23:12:28','2023-10-09 23:12:28'),(425,'trails',89,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Yellow Rock Trail, a steep and off-trail adventure that requires scrabbling and route-finding skills, starting from an unmarked parking area on Cottonwood Canyon Road about 14 miles north of US 89, crossing Cottonwood Creek, and following an eroded trail up a ravine to the right, to reach the broad, flat summit with sweeping views in all directions, where you can explore the colorful rocks, including a castle-like summit rock to the southwest, and admire the massive and magnificent dome of yellow sandstone with streaks of red, pink, and white.','2023-10-09 23:12:32','2023-10-09 23:12:32'),(426,'trails',96,'short',NULL,NULL,'Hike the Bull Valley Gorge Route to witness stunning geological formations and breathtaking views.','2023-10-09 23:12:32','2023-10-09 23:12:32');
